se`), so prayers stop all their damage. 0.1.36's log showed `MinibossGolem` with `IsBoss True`: minibosses count as bosses, so prayers halve their hits (the wiki says so). |\n\n## Milestone 0.3: faster builds and tests\n\nBuild, test and add content faster, without working the same things out again (`docs/design/milestones.md`, 0.3; the user's calls, 2026-10-21). From here: patch versions are checked with the fast local loop and scenarios, the two-run Melty test runs once per release, docs and the changelog once per release, and side sessions prep upcoming content while this session owns `src/`.\n\n| Status | Task | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Fast local loop** | `c42e589`: `python -I tools/loop.py <scenario.json ...> [--one-launch]` builds, installs into Megabonk (backing up `BepInEx/plugins/Bonkscape`), switches Melty's loader on, writes the scenario, launches `Megabonk.exe` directly, waits for the scenario's `done`, copies the log, report and screenshots to `derived/loop/`, closes only the PID it started and puts the plugin folder and the loader switch back. Measured: launch to run 10.5-12 s (14.8 s before the scenario start was cut from 3 s to 0.3 s), a boss-fight check 44 s end to end, six scenarios in one launch 249 s ([Testing](testing.md#the-local-loop)). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **The loop puts every save back** | Found 2026-10-22 checking `skills-saved` for the release: Claude's app package redirects only new AppData files, so the loop's game launches overwrote the user's real saves: Megabonk's (`stats.json`, `progression.json` in the Steam Cloud folder, `config.json`) and Bonkscape's skill saves, in 24 launches since the loop began (the docs had said the package isolated them). `tools/loop.py` now backs up Megabonk's `Saves` folder and Player logs and Bonkscape's skill saves, breadcrumbs and test rotation before each launch and restores them byte for byte with their times, removing files the run made; a killed loop restores on its next start (`derived/loop/PENDING-RESTORE.txt`). Proved by hashing all 15 files before and after scenario `saves` (5 changed by the run, all restored). What the earlier launches changed can't be undone (no copy from before them; Megabonk's `.bak` files hold only the previous save). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Scenario test mode** | `c42e589` and after: `Systems/Scenario.cs` reads `BepInEx/config/Bonkscape/scenario.json` (map, stage, adventurer, autoplay, boss_at, steps: spawn a monster or the stage boss, give a weapon, drop a potion or open a casket; preset levels, never saved; shots; expect; deaths; a `suite` of several in one launch). The scenario clock starts when the OSRS map is built. Plugin code: done with the 0.3 release's Melty test. **Tested in 0.3.0** (Melty `00b2a313`, graduated): both runs passed all 12 checks with no death (Morytania as the Mage, Lumbridge as the Ranger into the Mole's lair); `skills-saved` passed from `Skills.Tick`'s 20 s save. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Separate UI test** | The first version, as the plan asks (0.12 builds on it): scenario `ui` (about a minute, not run every time) sets each resolution in game and, on the menu, opens character select, map select and settings through Megabonk's own `MainMenu.GoTo*` calls, screenshotting each; on character select it checks Bonkscape's levels panel against every Megabonk text, icon and button there; in the run it screenshots the HUD and checks Bonkscape's boxes against the real minimap (`HudLayout.Boxes/Problems`); `bonk uilayout` does the HUD offline for eight resolutions. Found: the HUD measured Megabonk's minimap once (fixed: measured again on a resize); the special orb touches the minimap at 1280x720 (0.12); the levels panel covers Megabonk's character info at every resolution (0.6). A buttons-only first try passed that panel, hence the check against every graphic. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Dead-hook report** | `c42e589`: `Core/DeadHooks.cs` counts every patched method's calls in test runs (Melty's and scenarios) and lists the ones that never ran (scenario report; every 60 s in Melty runs). A 45 s Lumbridge run: 8 of 34 never ran, all situational (no chest opened, no death, no evade). Its first finding (0.3): `MapEdges.Set` never runs on Megabonk's Desert, so Morytania's arena took the map centre an earlier map left; every measured map is centred on the origin (the Forest's `MapEdges.Set` says 0,0), so a map without it is now centred there and the log says which. **Tested in 0.3.0** (Melty `00b2a313`, graduated): both runs passed all 12 checks with no death (Morytania as the Mage, Lumbridge as the Ranger into the Mole's lair); `skills-saved` passed from `Skills.Tick`'s 20 s save. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Deaths in test runs: the lethal-hit guard** | The user (2026-10-21): no blanket invulnerability, which would hide broken hits. `Systems/TestGuard.cs`: every hit lands in full through Megabonk's damage path; only one that would kill (`PlayerHealth.WillDamageKill`) is cut to leave 1 hitpoint, then the adventurer is healed to half and the save is logged with who hit and for how much; `PlayerHealth.PlayerDied` and `KillPlayer` prefixes stop any other way of dying (logged by name). On in Melty runs and scenarios; `\"deaths\": \"allow\"` keeps real deaths. New optional checks `player-hit` (a monster's hit cost health) and `run-restarted`. Scenario `guard`: 18 hits, 281 damage, 4 deaths saved, no restart; scenario `death`: the death screen and restart. A hit with no monster names Megabonk's damage source (`something (Enemy) hit for 30`: a Mole hit that arrives without the monster). The five scenarios in one launch: 233 s, every check passed, 0 warnings. `skills-saved` doesn't depend on a death: `Skills.Tick` saves every 20 s once XP changed (an extra 30-60 s save added in 0.3.0 on the belief that skills saved only at a run's end wa