nts wired). `Rows.Current`/`Bonk` make every system per map; test runs alternate maps. Superseded by the milestone plan: the Barrows stays Morytania's boss on its own map (0.7), and God Wars goes to the Forest's third stage (0.11), since Megabonk's Graveyard has only one stage. Next outlines by release: Al Kharid (0.8), the Wilderness (0.9), Ardougne & Seers' (0.10), the user's redraws replacing the default boxes whenever drawn. `maps.morytania` is `verified` since 0.2.4. **Tested in 0.2.1** (`008a84a6`, a publish-grade pass): the Mage's run on Morytania passed all 12 checks; footage shows Canifis, the *Tier 1 MORYTANIA* title, its monsters fighting, caskets and upgrades. |\n| <span class=\"st st-doing\">doing</span> | **9-tile performance work** | 0.2.1, for Morytania (56 regions, 10.1M faces in full): ground decorations and small non-blocking scenery left out on maps over 40,000 tiles (`[Map] LargeMapMinFaces` 300: 5.8M faces, Canifis reads the same in `bonk world`); the arena built on a background thread on the map's first run; Unity meshes made once per map and kept, scenery uploaded without a CPU copy; 19,000 box colliders grouped onto one object per chunk; scenery past `[Map] DrawDistance` (200 m) not drawn; minimap textures copied in one block. Shared vertices not done (triangle soup stays). 0.2.1's run: built in 1,977 ms off the main thread, then 4,668 ms placing it, 3,538 of them making meshes in one go (once per session; later runs reuse them). 0.2.2 makes the meshes a few a frame (8 ms budget) while the run waits, and the detail budget keeps tracked objects (it had dropped every ore rock: skilling found 5 plain rocks). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **OSRS projectiles and weapon sounds** | 0.1.36 (`8f67903`, test `a7436954` passed): each projectile carries its OSRS model (`Systems/Projectiles.cs`), the weapon's attack sound plays, monster hits and kills play `audio.enemy_hit` and `kill`. How the projectiles look hasn't been checked in footage. |\n\n## Recommended fixes and checks\n\n| Status | Task | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **The bosses table names a missing pet \"Dwarf remains\"** | `tools/wiki-site/gen_tables.py`'s bosses table looks up `pet_item_id` 0 as an item: Sir Lancelot's row reads *Dwarf remains, 1/0* on BSWiki's monsters page. A boss with `pet_item_id` 0 or `pet_rate` 0 should show *-*. Found by the 0.13.0 wiki update. |\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **Boss pets in a Melty run** | `5fe97177` showed clue trails in every run but no pet: autoplay never kills a boss in two minutes. A scenario with `\"pet\": \"drop\"` and a boss kill shows the drop, the follower and the pet line. Found by the 0.13.0 wiki update. |\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **Local suites hang at a scene load** | Seven local suites of 2026-10-22 stopped dead at a scene change: a run starting, a stage portal, the final stage (loop folders 08:01, 10:54, 13:34, 13:43, 13:55, 21:16 and 22:16). Megabonk stops responding and its CPU use drops to nothing, and Unity's `Player.log` always ends on the same line, `Unloading N Unused Serialized files` and its `UnloadTime`: the step before the scene load's `Unloading unused Assets`, which collects garbage. First blamed on the local-only music engine (the first two hung just after it began a track; since 0.12 scenarios run with the music off), but the last two played no OSRS music: 21:16 on the build before 0.15's osrs-kit switch, 22:16 on the switched one (its ninth scenario, `kbd-lair`, at `MapController.LoadFinalStage`). There: a 6.3 GB working set, 7 of 139 threads suspended, the main thread waiting, no crash dump and nothing in Windows' event log. A guess to check, not a finding: a garbage collection's stop-the-world that never ends. Melty's runs are one two-minute launch each, and every one that reached the KBD passed. Since 0.15 the loop calls a hang after 90 s without log output with the window not responding, writes `hang-threads.txt` (each thread's state, CPU and name) and `hang.dmp` (a minidump with every stack, for WinDbg) into its folder, and counts the suite's unfinished scenarios as failures. Next step: read a hang's threads; a Bonkscape thread among the suspended ones would make it ours. The triple check's code pass found no Bonkscape thread alive at the hangs and no unload or finalizer code of ours; its leads: `OsrsAudio.OnAudioFilterRead`, managed code on Unity's audio thread, attached whether or not music plays (test: a switch that never attaches it, and FMOD's mixer among the suspended threads); the phantom run a suite's boundary started on the dying stage (fixed in `b2cd0af`, but it can't explain the 22:16 hang inside a run). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **0.7's sheet statuses** | At `508f097` the Barrows rows are still `status: \"todo\"`: the six brothers' `characters` rows, Guthan's, Karil's, Torag's and Verac's `enemies` rows, the brothers' six `weapons`, the 24 `drops` pieces, and Morytania's `waves` (rows that were `built` before 0.7 went back to `todo`). BSWiki's generated tables call them planned (phase 1) although 0.7.0 plays them and Melty test `4b4dffee` passed. Raise them and run `gen_tables.py`. Found by the 0.7.0 wiki update (`6d1791c`). Raised (`243665d`): those rows `built`, Torag's `enemies` row and Morytania's waves `verified` (seen in `4b4dffee` run 2); `gen_tables.py` run. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Prayer's log says \"half blocked\" for a full block** | `Prayer`'s damage prefix labels every boss hit `boss, half blocked`, also one `bosses.prayer_blocks_fully` blocks completely (Jad), so 0.7.0's footage review reported Jad's prayed hits as half blocked. Label the outcome by the block applied. Found by the 0.7.0 wiki update. `243665d`: the outcome names the block applied (`boss, fully blocked` for Jad, `boss, half blocked` for the rest). Melty test with the next version. In 0.8.0 (*boss, fully blocked* for Jad), whose Melty test `7b1d13bb` passed. |\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **The Barrows rewards, the Queen's second form and the KBD's breaths in a Melty run** | BSWiki marks unconfirmed what `4b4dffee` couldn't show: a set effect triggering, the chest's piece after the hidden brother dies, a full set unlocking its brother, a brother played. Autoplay never kills a stage boss in two minutes; the scenarios (`morytania-barrows`, `barrows-sets-*`, `barrows-dharok`) cover them locally. Found by the 0.7.0 wiki update. 0.8.0's `7b1d13bb` didn't reach the Kalphite Queen's second form either (autoplay had her for about 30 s); `kalphite-lair` shows it locally. 0.9.1's `5816c823` met the King Black Dragon in his lair but not his breaths' poison, freeze or shock, nor his death ending the run; `kbd-lair` shows them locally. |\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **Deep Freeze's ice block draws solid** | 0.6.0's Melty test `b459ddbb` (run 2, 02:00): the ice block Ice Barrage's spec leaves on its target (spotanim 369) draws as an opaque tan box, not OSRS's see-through ice. Found in the footage review; noted in BSWiki's known limitations (`bd1dd52`). Also noted on the *OSRS looks for the new weapons' base attacks* row. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **A starting-weapon upgrade can be weaker than the default** | `Content.StartingWeapon` takes any met requirement over the row's own weapon, so Durial321 at 60-69 Attack starts with Dragon claws instead of his Abyssal whip (and the Void Knight loses his mace at 60 Attack). Decide whether an upgrade must beat the default's own requirement. Found by the 0.6.0 wiki update. Decided and built (`4fdfeaf`): an upgrade must ask more than the row's own weapon, so Durial321 keeps his whip (Attack 70) until a weapon asking more; a \"starting_weapon\" default (scimitar, shortbow, Fire Strike, the Void knight mace) asks nothing, so the skills upgrade it as for the Warrior. Melty test `4b4dffee` of 0.7.0 passed (graduates; 3 runs: Lumbridge, Morytania, TzHaar City): the Void Knight started with his mace and the level-up offers listed it at level 1 (run 3, 00:22 and 01:22); the Warrior's skills still upgraded his scimitar to the Dragon scimitar (run 1). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **`hooks.unlock_override`'s purpose text** | `sheets/hooks.json` still says \"Bonkscape's weapons, tomes and adventurers count as unlocked\"; since 0.6.0 an adventurer follows its `unlock_condition` (`Unlocks.CharacterUnlocked`), so the Ironman stays locked until total level 250. Update the row; BSWiki's generated hooks table follows. Found by the 0.6.0 wiki update. Updated in `4fdfeaf`; the generated hooks table follows. |\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **Spec keys pressed by hand** | 0.4: tests fire specs through `Specials.Fire` and the switch through `Specials.Switch` (autoplay and the scenario `spec` step); the keys (`[Special] FireKey` F, `SwitchKey` Q, read with `Input.GetKeyDown`) call the same two methods but haven't been pressed in a run. One hand test, or a check once 0.16 moves the keys to Rewired. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Scenario suites ran every scenario as the first one's adventurer** | Found 2026-10-22 (0.4): `MapController.StartNewMap` in a run keeps the player, so 0.3's suites (the five-scenario regression too) played each later scenario with the first one's adventurer and weapons; their timings hold, their per-adventurer checks didn't test what they said. Suites now go back to the main menu between scenarios (`TransitionUI.LoadMenu`), about 5 s each, and every scenario reports and checks who is playing. Also: a suite's screenshots were left in the game folder (collected now; 36 old ones moved to `derived/loop/leftover-suite-shots`). |\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **OSRS looks for the new weapons' base attacks** | 0.4's spec scenarios: the Armadyl godsword attacks with Megabonk's Hero Sword blade wave (a big pale-blue blade crossing the screen) and Ice Barrage leaves Frostwalker's ice ring, Megabonk's own visuals for the base weapons. `Systems/Projectiles.cs` gives projectiles OSRS models; these are effects it doesn't reach. 0.6.0's test: Ice Barrage's special now puts the ice block (spot animation 369) on its target, but it draws as an opaque tan box where OSRS shows see-through pale ice (the flipbook render keeps neither the faces' transparency nor the right colour). |\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **Settings window open on the menu in a Melty run** | 0.3.0's Melty run 2 (`00b2a313`, 00:12-00:14): Megabonk's Settings window was open on the main menu before autoplay started the run (run 1 showed the plain menu); the run was unaffected. Check whether Megabonk reopens the last menu tab from its config (the UI test opens settings through `MainMenu.GoToSettings`, and the loop now restores `config.json`) or something else opened it. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Stripped-method gate** | 0.1.22: `bonk unstripped <Bonkscape.dll> <interopDir>` lists every game or Unity method the plugin calls that Il2CppInterop rebuilt instead of binding natively; `tools/package.py` refuses to package when one isn't on its known-safe list (five pure-maths or thin calls that ran in passing tests). It already caught `AudioSource.mute` in the music hookup. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Per-feature Melty test checks** | In the recipe since 0.1.31-0.1.33: `monster-animated`, `adventurer-animated`, `prayer-blocked`, `skills-saved`, `evolution` (12 checks, all passed in 0.1.35); `skilling-gathered` is written when it happens. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **README in the release zip** | 0.1.30 (`5ffe5f3`): a Settings table naming every config key with its default, the HUD and per-adventurer skills described; `tools/package.py` refuses a README that doesn't name every `Config.Bind` key as `[Section] Key`. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Giant Mole fight in the Mole lair** | The user changed their mind (2026-10-21): \"for giant mole, boss fight should teleport you to mole cave with digging behavior\". 0.2.10 (Melty test `ae55a279`, passed, both runs counted): summoning the Mole moves the fight into the Falador Mole Lair (`bosses.osrs_arena`) under the loading cover in 31 ms, the Mole on its own tile, a black cave sky; its dig resurfaces it 8 to 18 steps' walk away, never across a wall. The run showed the adventurer climbing the cave walls' flat tops and falling into the void twice (caught by the fall net): 0.2.11 walls off every blocked tile beside the floor and moves monster spawns off the walls onto the floor (test `036cdc9a`: no more climbing; one fall left, through a ground-less stone-floored patch reached under its raised walls); 0.2.12 makes ground-less tiles unwalkable and walls them off on every map (test `80711319`, passed, both runs counted: no falls; dying in the lair restarts on Lumbridge with its sky back). Still Megabonk's boss stats. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Canifis stilt houses had no walls** | The user (2026-10-21): \"morytania buildings that are raised are not rendering correctly, they show no walls\". A stilt house's walls sit on the level-1 tiles just outside its bridge floor, which the builder dropped as an upstairs floor (32 of 34 walls on one house). 0.2.11 takes level-1 walls and wall decorations next to a bridge tile: 1,217 come back on Morytania (190 around Canifis), none change on Lumbridge. `bonk world` renders show every Canifis house walled; 0.2.11's Melty test (`036cdc9a`, run 1) shows them walled in game. |\n| <span class=\"st st-todo\">todo</span> | **Back to the surface after the Mole?** | Gameplay call for the user, not asked: today you stay in the lair after the kill; a restart after dying there is back on Lumbridge (0.2.12's test). Unverified: where Megabonk's next-stage portal appears after the kill (no test has killed the Mole). If it sits on Megabonk's hidden Forest stage, a player could be stranded in the lair, which would make \"climb out after the kill\" (as OSRS has you climb the rope) the fix. Needs a play-through to the Mole's death. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Monster rosters per map from NPC spawn data** | `tools/mapideas/rosters.py` writes `docs/design/rosters.md`: attackable spawns inside every map's area and the user's outlines, against the enemies sheet. Lumbridge: every sheet monster lives there except the guard and the Giant Mole; frogs, big frogs, giant spiders and men have no row yet. Map 1: 527 spawns of 41 kinds. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Hand-over playtest mode** | 0.1.33 (`c751a15`): `Dev.ManualTest = true` makes Melty test runs leave the playing to you while Bonkscape still writes its self-checks (log: `Melty test mode: hand-over`). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Colours paler than OSRS** | 0.1.34 (`c7c7d98`): Megabonk renders in Linear colour space (0.1.33's log), where OSRS's gamma vertex colours came out paler; every OSRS mesh's colours now go through gamma-to-linear. 0.1.35's footage shows deeper greens and stone. Textured faces are still one average colour. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Autoplay hugging walls** | 0.1.33: a two-tile probe on Lumbridge's grid (`CanStep`, walls on tile edges) turns autoplay toward open ground. 0.1.35's log showed it never ran, read then as IL2CPP inlining `PlayerInput.MovementInput`; 0.15's lane A found the real cause: since 0.1.33 the `[HarmonyPatch]` attribute sat detached from its class, so the patch was never applied (removed in `4db9e87`). 0.1.36 (`8f67903`) writes the movement in LateUpdate instead. Confirmed in 0.2.1's test (`008a84a6`): the log counts wall turns (6 to 36 every 30 s on Lumbridge) and the Warrior's recording shows it roaming the fields and Lumbridge castle's hall instead of pressing into walls. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **`hooks.json` behind the code** | 0.1.30 (`5ffe5f3`) and 0.1.31: rows for every patch (39 rows, including `weapon_used`, `player_evade`, `player_aegis`), patch kinds matching the code; the preflight checks each target exists (phase 1 open 0). The `icons_*`, `music` and `timer` rows are data/poll rows by design. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **HUD sprites in the `ui` sheet** | 0.1.35 (`c5cdfec`): rows for every interface sprite the HUD draws by id (orbs, XP counter, minimap frame, compass, prayer glow, health bars) and the q8 dialogue font; the preflight checks them in a player's cache (202 assets, phase 1 open 0). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Sheet statuses that don't match the code** | 0.1.33-0.1.35: `ui.run_timer` (cut in 0.1.7) moved to phase 3; poison hitsplats now show for Megabonk's poison; the skilling drops' `effect` strings drive the rewards (`maps.skilling_nodes` names each node's drop). `skills.unlocks` vs `weapons.gear_reqs` belongs to the skipped gear requirements. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Bonkscape's data edits on other maps** | Decided (the user, 2026-10-21): keep all Bonkscape behaviour wherever the mod is loaded, so the OSRS names, stats and pools stay on Megabonk's other maps too. That is how the code already works; nothing to change. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Skilling spots aren't marked** | 0.1.34 (`c7c7d98`): each usable spot near the adventurer shows its skill's OSRS icon (gone while it respawns) and a progress bar while gathering; ore rocks are tracked by name (copper, tin, coal in Lumbridge) and mining names the ore. |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Docs checks in the release steps** | `tools/package.py` runs `check_docs.py --head --release` (every check but the version one, whose changelog entry comes after the build and test) and refuses to build the zip on any FAIL (`--skip-docs` for emergencies, with a warning). The checker now also fails on a patch-table row with no patch in the code (the stale `EnemyHpBar.Set` case), and `docs_state.json` no longer counts as a changed source. The prose side is the `wiki-update` skill (`.claude/skills/wiki-update/`): run it after each version's build and test ([How to update these docs](updating-docs.md#the-docs-checker)). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Docket kept with every commit** | `2fdbddf`: after every commit to this repo, from any session, `.claude/hooks/bonkscape_after_commit.py` (a Claude Code hook in `~/.claude/settings.json`) asks the committing session to update its docket rows and reports `check_docs.py --head --release`, which now checks the docket too (statuses, duplicate tasks, paths, the \"Last updated\" line) ([With every commit](updating-docs.md#with-every-commit)). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | **Add the inlining and colour-space gotchas to MODLOG** | 0.2.4: MODLOG gotchas 20 (methods IL2CPP inlined: `WeaponBase.Use`, `InteractableChest.OpenChestImplementation`, `PlayerInput.MovementInput`), 21 (Linear colour space) and 22 (the Barrows brothers' pink NPC models). |\n| <span class=\"st st-doing\">doing</span> | **Check the new visuals in footage** | Seen in the 0.2.0-0.2.3 recordings: monster attacks and deaths (corpses lying where they fell), the adventurers' walk, run and weapon attacks, rune arrows at arm length after the 0.2.1 fix, deep OSRS greens and stone. Still unseen: skilling markers (autoplay doesn't skill). |\n\n## Done\n\n| Status | Task | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | The crash fix and the OSRS HUD | 0.1.22 to 0.1.26: the start crash fixed, the full OSRS HUD running, health bars, hitsplats on bodies, the protection sparkle (see Current push) |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | The hub | One launcher for the tools: `Bonkscape Hub.cmd` or the Desktop shortcut starts `tools/hub` (port 8789), which starts the wiki and sketch servers and links them and the other docs. Since `bc5456c` it runs on the generic project-hub engine (`../project-hub`), with everything Bonkscape-specific in `tools/hub/hub.json`. BSWiki is online (public) at jacksonarnold27.github.io/bswiki from the `bswiki` repo; `export_wiki.py --push` updates it ([The hub](hub.md#hosting-bswiki)). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | Playability pass | 0.1.12: pathing, roomier collision, bigger adventurer, prayer bar |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | Map sketch tool | `tools/sketch` with surface, underground and instance views. Map names can be typed again (`5643de2`: selecting a map rebuilt the list and dropped the name box's focus). |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | Full cache on this PC | The RuneLite cache replaced with the full archive (backup kept); preflight 143/143 phase-1 assets; `bonk worldscan` |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | The real Lumbridge | 0.1.7 to 0.1.11; passed the publish test |\n| <span class=\"st st-done\">done</span> | Phase 1 systems | 0.1.0 to 0.1.5; passed the publish test |\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/ sheets/ -->\n"}, {"section": "dev", "slug": "gotchas", "text": "# Gotchas\n\nHard-won lessons, grouped by topic. Numbers in brackets are the numbered gotchas in `docs/MODLOG.md` (keep both in\nstep: MODLOG is the short log, this page explains). Unnumbered ones are on this page only.\n\n## Melty and the loader\n\n- **Melty reads at most 12 check lines per run.** Write only the recipe's `test.checks` to the checks file: an optional\n  check written early pushes a required one past the limit, and the run doesn't count although the log shows it passed\n  (0.2.0 run 2, 0.2.5 run 1). `TestMode.Pass` writes recipe checks only.\n- **Melty must be started by the user, not from Claude's shell** [1]. `Start-Process melty://...` from the Claude\n  desktop app runs Melty inside the app's MSIX package; its AppData writes (the test file, `settings.properties`) land in\n  `Packages/Claude_*/LocalCache`, invisible to Megabonk, which Steam starts.\n- **Melty disables BepInEx between its own launches** [2] (renames `winhttp.dll` to `winhttp.dll.melty-off`). Iterate\n  with `test_release` runs rather than launching Megabonk directly.\n- **The BepInEx build Melty installs isn't the listed one.** The listing says 6.0.0-pre.2; the installed build is\n  **6.0.0-be.697** (.NET 6.0.7). Compile against `BepInEx/core` from the game folder (`lib/bepinex-melty`), not the\n  GitHub pre.2 zip.\n- **Interop assemblies only exist after a first Melty test launch** (`BepInEx/interop`). Without them the plugin builds\n  without its game code (`INTEROP` undefined), and you get a stub.\n- **`{game:custom-old-school-runescape}` is the Steam OSRS folder, which has no cache.** That's why the plugin searches\n  RuneLite's and the official client's cache folders itself.\n- **Self-checks are read line by line from the checks file**, verdict first: `pass osrs-cache` or `fail osrs-cache: why`.\n  `osrs-cache=pass` or a bare name aren't read. List in the recipe's `test.checks` only names the mod reports (0.1.0's\n  test failed with every check missing).\n- **A 1-run test is a quick check**; only a test of 2+ runs with every self-check passing in each run lets a new mashup\n  be published.\n\n## IL2CPP and Harmony\n\n- **Stripped Unity members** [3]. Members Megabonk never uses are missing, and calling them throws *\"Method unstripping\n  failed\"* (0.1.1's test failed on it). Missing: `AudioClip.Create/SetData` (all runtime clip creation),\n  `Renderer.forceRenderingOff`, `shadowCastingMode`, `receiveShadows`, `AudioSource.playOnAwake/spatialBlend`,\n  `ScreenCapture.CaptureScreenshot`. Present: `Mesh.vertices/colors32/triangles`, `Texture2D.LoadRawTextureData`,\n  `Renderer.enabled`, `GUI.DrawTexture`. Before relying on a member, scan the interop assemblies for\n  `throw new NotSupportedException(\"Method unstripping failed\")`.\n- **Rebuilt members crash natively** [19]. Some stripped members aren't missing: Il2CppInterop *rebuilds* them from\n  Unity's reference code, and a rebuilt copy can disagree with the game's Unity. `GUI.DrawTextureWithTexCoords` did:\n  it smashed the stack (coreclr `c0000005`) on the first HUD frame of every run, 0.1.17-0.1.21. A bound member has a\n  `NativeMethodInfoPtr_<name>` field in its interop type; a rebuilt one doesn't. `bonk unstripped <Bonkscape.dll>\n  <interopDir>` lists every rebuilt member the plugin calls, and `tools/package.py` refuses to package when there is\n  one not on its known-safe list. Draw part of a sprite from its own cut texture (`UnityConv.SpritePart`) instead.\n- **Sound without AudioClips** [4]: OSRS sounds are synthesised and mixed into the output from an injected\n  MonoBehaviour's `OnAudioFilterRead` on the AudioListener's object, re-attached when scenes replace the camera.\n- **Harmony on IL2CPP virtual methods recurses** [5]. Calling the original from a patch goes back through virtual\n  dispatch into the patch: stack overflow. Never let Harmony run the original of a virtual/override (`GetIcon`,\n  `GetDescription`). Set data fields instead (`WeaponData.icon`, `TomeData.description`), or reimplement and return\n  false (`GetName` reads `localizedName` itself).\n- **`ReadOnlySpan` overloads** [6] in interop (`AudioClip.SetData`, `Il2CppStructArray` constructors) break overload\n  resolution and reflection lookups. Cast sizes to `long`; avoid reflection over those methods.\n- **Name collisions** [11] with Megabonk's global types: `MeshData` (alias it inside the namespace), the `Assets`\n  namespace.\n- **Static initialisers that touch Unity can kill a whole class.** 0.1.0's test logged *\"The type initializer for\n  'Bonkscape.Core.UnityConv' threw an exception\"* on every use. Create Unity objects lazily, on the main thread.\n\n- **IL2CPP inlines small methods, and a patch on an inlined method never runs** [20]. Harmony patches the method, but\n  its callers carry their own copy. Two Bonkscape hooks never fired: `WeaponBase.Use` for weapon attacks (0.1.31;\n  `WeaponAttack.SuccessfullySpawnedProjectile` works) and `InteractableChest.OpenChestImplementation` for caskets\n  (0.1.33; `ChestWindowUi.OpenButton` works). Give every hook a counter or a self-check, and prefer public entry points.\n- **A `[HarmonyPatch]` attribute that slips off its class patches nothing, and nothing says so** [23]. 0.1.33 put a\n  new method between `[HarmonyPatch(typeof(PlayerInput), \"MovementInput\")]` and the patch class under it; the attribute\n  then sat on a method of `TestMode`, `PatchAll` skipped it, and the postfix was never applied. 0.1.35 took the\n  silence for inlining and moved autoplay's movement into `TestMode.LateTick`, where it still is (0.15 removed the\n  orphaned patch; whether `MovementInput` is inlined was never tested). The dead-hook report can't see it: it counts\n  only methods that were patched (`counting calls to N patched methods` in the log), and `check_docs`' `[patches]` check\n  reads the attribute's text. Keep the attribute directly on its class, and compare N with the distinct targets in\n  [Architecture's patch table](architecture.md#harmony-patches).\n\n## Megabonk runtime\n\n- **Megabonk's pause menu doesn't stop `Time.timeScale`.** The level-up offers and the encounters do. To know a\n  Megabonk window is up, look for an active `Window` component (pause, chest, encounters), `LevelupScreen.isLevelingUp`\n  and the death screen's `deadUiWindow` (`Hud.MegabonkWindowUp`).\n- **Megabonk's `MainMenu.SetWindow` toggles.** Going to the main tab while it's open switches it off, and the menu then\n  shows no window at all. 0.12's UI test did that with a trailing return to the menu, and the title screen seemed to\n  vanish after a resolution change. A return to the menu now checks the main tab first.\n- **More of uGUI and Unity is stripped than the HUD needed so far** (checked with probes, 0.12): `CanvasGroup`'s\n  `interactable` and `blocksRaycasts` setters (its `alpha` is fine), `Sprite.Create`, `Texture2D.Apply(bool)`,\n  `ImageConversion.LoadImage`, `GUI.BeginGroup`/`BeginClip`, `Graphics.CopyTexture` with a region, `Graphics.Blit`\n  with a scale and offset, `RenderTexture.GetTemporary`. Bound and safe: `RawImage`, the `Image` setters,\n  `Behaviour.enabled`, `SetPixels32`, `LoadRawTextureData`, `new RenderTexture`, the plain `Graphics.Blit`,\n  `ReadPixels`, `GetPixels32`. The title screen stops clicks on Megabonk's menu by switching its canvas's\n  `GraphicRaycaster` off; setting the menu tab's own `CanvasGroup` alpha was dropped as unneeded.\n- **Ent3 hovers.** Megabonk's Forest final boss (*Bark Vader*, the God Wars generals' ride) floats as it closes in:\n  its enemy object was 15-18 m above the floor while the OSRS model's feet stayed on it (`EnemySkins` plants them).\n  With a 3D distance from the adventurer, no general was ever beside them, so Zilyana never attacked and K'ril never\n  used his melee in 0.11's first runs. `BossFight` measures reach across the floor from the model's feet\n  (`Flat`, `Feet`), and projectiles leave from the model too.\n- **A game started from Claude's shell writes the user's real saves**: Claude's desktop app is an MSIX package, and\n  programs its shell starts see AppData through it, but it redirects only **new** files (into\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Packages\\Claude_*\\LocalCache`, seen only from inside the package). Overwriting a file that already\n  exists writes the real one. Megabonk saves in place (`LocalLow\\Ved\\Megabonk\\Saves\\CloudDir\\<steam id>\\stats.json`,\n  `progression.json`, synced by Steam Cloud; `LocalDir\\config.json`), and `Skills.Save` writes a new `.tmp` (redirected)\n  then copies it over the existing `skills_<adventurer>.json` (real). The 0.3 loop first assumed the package isolated\n  them (a probe file, a new file, landed in `LocalCache`), and 24 launches on 2026-10-21/22 changed the real saves before\n  that was caught; since then the loop backs every save up before a launch and restores it byte for byte after\n  ([Testing](testing.md#the-local-loop)). The scenario and report live in the game folder (`BepInEx/config/Bonkscape`),\n  which every process sees.\n- **`MapController.StartNewMap` always starts on the map's first stage**: its `RunConfig.stageData` isn't read, and\n  `MapController.TestMap` starts nothing (the loop timed out waiting). `MapController.GetStageIndex()` counts the\n  stages played in the run, not which stage is loaded: find `MapController.currentStage` in `currentMap.stages`\n  (`Bonk.StageIndex`). A test run on a later stage starts on the first and moves on with `MapController.index` set to\n  the stage before it and `LoadNextStage()` (`TestMode.JumpToStage`), as Megabonk's own stage end does; the scenarios\n  that said `\"stage\": 1` or `2` before 0.5 played stage 0.\n- **A map's last stage has a different boss portal**: `InteractableBossSpawnerFinal` (`GenerateTileObjects.bossSpawnerFinal`),\n  not `InteractableBossSpawner`, which it doesn't derive from. Opening it summons the stage's final boss in place, as the\n  other does (Pharaoh3, `IsFinalBoss`, about 10 s later). TzHaar City is the Desert's third and last stage, so 0.5's\n  first Fight Caves runs found \"no boss portal\": the test boss call and the arena's portal placement take both.\n- **Megabonk scales a monster's hp and hits by stage, and its `CombatScaling` getters are inlined**: a monster's hp is\n  its base x `RunConfig.GetEnemyHp` (0.8) x (1 + `CombatScaling.GetStageHpMultiplier()`), and that stage share is 0 on\n  a map's first stage and 145 on the Desert's third (hits: about 4 more). A run that gets there brings two stages of\n  levels and upgrades; a test run that jumped there brings none, and 0.5's first TzHaar City run met a TzHaar-Mej with\n  15 hp in the sheet and 1,752 in the game. Postfixes on `GetStageHpMultiplier` / `GetStageDamageMultiplier` never ran\n  (inlined, as above), so jumped test runs divide the share back out themselves: `TestMode.StageScale`, applied to each\n  monster's hp as it spawns (`EnemySkins`) and to each hit on the adventurer (`Prayer`). A player's run keeps\n  Megabonk's scaling.\n- **Megabonk's Desert stages share their enemy types** (Mummy, Scorpion, the sand golem... on all three; only the\n  bosses differ: Pharaoh1, 2, 3), so two maps on one Megabonk map key their monsters by map row (`Rows.EnemyByMapBonk`)\n  and the stage's rows go onto the shared `EnemyData` when it starts (`Content.ApplyStage`).\n- **`PlayerHealth.Heal` does nothing at 0 hp.** 0.5's first Fight Caves run lost the adventurer to a death the guard in\n  `Damage` didn't catch (after a TzHaar-Ket champion, Megabonk's sand golem, joined), and the backstop's heal then left\n  them at 0: \"healed to 0/105\", 74 times over. The backstop now sets `hp` itself. Some of Megabonk's hits carry no\n  monster (`dc.enemy` null: the champion's slam hit for 91), and its second damage call, `DamagePlayerExternal` (a\n  bare amount, used by shooters' projectiles), goes on to `Damage`.\n- **Most of a boss's or miniboss's hits name no monster**: their projectiles and slams reach `PlayerHealth.Damage` with\n  `dc.enemy` null (the log's \"something hit for 4\"), so nothing ties them to the brother who threw them. 0.7's set\n  effects take the nearest brother within 10 m as the hitter (`SetEffects.NearestBrother`); since 0.14.2 a protection\n  prayer takes the nearest live boss within 30 m (`Prayer.NearestBoss`: the Giant Mole's roots, Megabonk's Ent\n  `RootAttackSinglePredictive`, reach the adventurer from 20 m and more and had gone unblocked), and test autoplay prays\n  against that boss first. A scenario's spawn step skins as the row it names (`EnemySkins.NextRow`); before 0.7\n  six brothers spawned on Pharaoh1 all came out as the first row on it.\n- **A monster can't be hurt for about its first second** (`Enemy.CanTakeDamage()` false): every hit then is ignored,\n  Bonkscape's and Megabonk's alike, and `hp` doesn't change. When it can, `DamageFromPlayerWeapon` takes the hp off at\n  once (armour first: `armorCurrent`). 0.4's first spec tests fired 0.5 s after a spawn and looked like specs did no\n  damage; specs now target only monsters that can take damage.\n- **`MapController.StartNewMap` in a run keeps the player**: like a stage change, the next run has the same adventurer\n  and inventory (`MapController.inventory`; `TryCleanupInventory` leaves it, and clearing it isn't enough either). A\n  real new run goes through the main menu (`TransitionUI.Instance.LoadMenu()`), as scenario suites do since 0.4.\n- **A suite's last run can start a stage again after the next scenario loads** (0.6: smoke's Morytania run started\n  once more as weapon-ranger loaded, and \"playing as Mage, not Ranger\" was judged on it). `Scenario.Tick` waits for the\n  scenario's own run request (`TestMode.RunRequested`).\n- **A character is applied before the player's inventory names it** (`PlayerRenderer.SetCharacter` comes first, and\n  Runner's stage start measures before both), so `Sizing.Measure` takes the character from `PlayerSkin.Apply`; until\n  0.6 the first measure had no name and a switch between runs was never seen.\n- **Megabonk puts its saved character back into the selection as the menu opens**\n  (`SaveManager.config.preferences.selectedCharacter`): a scenario starting 0.3 s after the menu sometimes had its\n  `CharacterMenu.selectedCharacter` overwritten and played the saved one (two of 0.4's suite runs: the Mage asked for,\n  the Warrior played). Scenario runs set the preference too; Melty's runs start 3 s in and never hit it.\n- **A setting's default is written into the config on first launch**: changing the default in `Config.Bind` later\n  doesn't change a config that already has the key (0.4's `NearPitch`: 35, then 45). Edit the `.cfg`, or delete the\n  key, to try a new default.\n- **Commit a merge at once in this shared checkout**: other sessions commit here, and a commit made while a merge is\n  staged becomes the merge commit (0.4: the planning session's docket commit `b429736` carries the `specials-0.4`\n  merge).\n- **Megabonk ignores Unity's `-screen-fullscreen/-screen-width/-screen-height` flags** and runs at its own settings;\n  the UI test sets sizes in game with `Screen.SetResolution`, which clamps to the desktop (3840x2160 became\n  3840x1934 on a 2560x1600 screen).\n- **`ScreenCapture.CaptureScreenshot` and `ImageConversion.EncodeToPNG` are stripped from Megabonk** (`bonk\n  unstripped`), and the interop has only the generic `GetRawTextureData<T>`: screenshots are `ReadPixels` in an IMGUI\n  repaint, `GetPixels32`, and a PNG written by the plugin (`Systems/Shot.cs`).\n- **A boss summoned before the arena is built never gets its lair**: `Arena.BossSummoned` needs `Arena.Active`.\n  Players can't reach the portal that fast, but a scenario spawning at 3 s could (a suite's later runs build their\n  arena in 3-4 s): scenario clocks start once the arena is built.\n\n- **Megabonk's transforms are scaled and rotated for its own art** [7]: enemy and player renderer bounds report about\n  4.5 m. OSRS models are separate objects that follow their owner (positioned every `LateUpdate`, facing the player or\n  the movement), not children of Megabonk's model.\n- **Level-up and shrine windows pause the game** [8] (`deltaTime` 0). Autoplay timers use unscaled time; press the\n  first `UpgradeButton.SelectUpgrade()`, and `BaseEncounterWindow.ChooseOffer(0)` for shrine windows.\n- **The player object appears a moment after the run starts** [9]: start-of-run setup keys off `MyPlayer.Instance`,\n  and a new player object means a new stage.\n- **The Forest stage's structure** [15]: 600 m square centred on (0, 10, 0), built from generated tile prefabs\n  (`ProceduralTileGeneration.tilesParent/newRoot/collider`) plus prop roots. Layers: Ground 8, Object 12, Water 4,\n  TransparentFX 1 (tree camera colliders), minimap 14. Camera collision is 1\\|8\\|12; spawns raycast Ground\\|Object\n  inside `SpawnPositions.minX..maxZ`, which `FindBounds` recomputes, so override it in a postfix. A catch box\n  `WorldCollider` sits at y -60 (top about -30). (Learned from the 0.1.6 diagnostic build.)\n- **Moving the player** [16]: `TeleportPlayerImmediate`/`TeleportPlayer` alone don't stick during the spawn intro.\n  Wait until `!isTeleporting && grounded`, then set `playerMovement.rb.position` (velocity 0) and the transform, and\n  repeat for a moment. (0.1.7 and 0.1.8 built the arena but never reported a kill, pickup, prayer or XP drop; 0.1.8\n  added this wait, and 0.1.9, with re-wound ground triangles, was the first arena build to pass.)\n- **Megabonk's bosses and minibosses** are recognised in `Content.ApplyEnemies` as `Miniboss*` names or\n  `maps == None && hp >= 1000`; their stats are left alone.\n- **Calling off an enemy's death** (0.8, the Kalphite Queen's second form): a prefix on `Enemy.EnemyDied(DamageContainer)`\n  returning false keeps it, but by then Megabonk has flagged it (`isDyingNextFrame`, `deathFunctionCalled`) and its\n  health is 0, so `SecondForm` clears both flags and sets `hp` and `controlHp` back to `maxHp`. Harmony still runs the\n  postfixes of a skipped original, so Combat's death postfix asks `SecondForm.TakeCalledOff` first. Megabonk's boss\n  bar keeps its old number until the next hit, and turns blue while the boss is `MakeInvulnerable(true)`.\n- **Two enemies rows on one Megabonk enemy of one map: the last one listed takes the slot** (`Rows.EnemyByMapBonk`).\n  The Queen's airborne row, after the crawling one on `Pharaoh1`, took it, so she spawned airborne (no lair, no second\n  form). A boss's `second_form` is left out of the slot lookups (`Rows.Build`).\n- **Megabonk's Graveyard starts in a crypt** (0.9): a maze far from the surface, \"Find the exit\". The stage clock\n  and its monsters start only when its exit door (`InteractableCryptLeave`) is used: moved out by a teleport, the\n  run stayed on \"Find the exit\" with no monsters for three minutes. Its boss is reached through coffins, crypt keys,\n  the surface `InteractableCrypt` and the Ghost King's room of lamps (`GraveyardBossRoom`, which isn't in the scene\n  until then); `MapController.LoadFinalStage` instead loads Megabonk's final-boss map (`MapGenerationFinalBoss`),\n  whose final boss is **Ent1** (the dump's stage `boss`), and its death ends the run.\n- **`GoldenSkeleton` runs from the player** (`isRunningFromPlayer` in the dump): nothing that should fight can ride it.\n- **A pooled enemy keeps whatever was keyed to its pointer:** Megabonk reused the dead KBD's enemy for a ghoul, which\n  went on breathing his dragonfire until `EnemySkins.OnSpawn` cleared `BossFight`'s scripted state (as `SecondForm`'s).\n- **`MapController.LoadFinalStage` under a pause loads a frozen stage:** with time scale 0 (a level-up or reward\n  window open) the final stage stays at time scale 0 with Megabonk's cinematic bars up and no boss, for good (0.9's\n  `kbd-paused` experiment; 0.9.0's Melty run 1 went black when test mode opened the Graveyard's boss door then).\n  Test mode's boss call and the boss door both wait for the pause to end.\n- **Two maps' first stages can share a Megabonk boss** (Lumbridge's Mole and the Wilderness's KBD both on Ent1): every\n  row applied at load names its Megabonk enemy, the last one wins, so 0.10.0's Melty run showed the Mole as \"King Black\n  Dragon\". `Content.ApplyStage` now runs for every stage a launch plays, its first included (`Content.StageApplied`),\n  and never applies a PKer's row (it would put the PKer's walker in the map's pool).\n\n## OSRS cache and models\n\n- **OSRS item models lie flat.** Fit ground items and projectiles by their longest side (`UnityConv.Longest`,\n  `AttachFit`), not their height: 0.2.0 drew rune arrows metres long and single coins as big gold discs.\n- **Players' caches are partial** (the client downloads models on demand), hence stand-ins and graceful fallbacks\n  everywhere. The creator's PC sets `[OSRS] CacheFolder` to an archived full cache for testing, and its RuneLite cache\n  has the full archive too.\n- **OSRS NPC models need their standing frame** [12] or they T-pose. Lighting is computed before the pose and the NPC\n  scale after it, as the client does.\n- **Facing** [10]: OSRS models face +z after flipping OSRS y and z; since `UnityConv` also flips z (0.1.7, next\n  point), they face -z (south) in Unity, so `EnemySkins` and `PlayerSkin` use a 180 degree `YawOffset`.\n- **Handedness** [13]. OSRS axes (x east, y down, z north) are right-handed; Unity is left-handed. `MeshData` keeps\n  OSRS's frame with y and z flipped (a rotation), so `UnityConv` flips z once more (a reflection), or every model is\n  mirrored (the sword in the wrong hand, the River Lum west of the castle). A reflection reverses winding, so triangles\n  are re-wound: Unity mesh colliders and raycasts are one-sided, and ground facing down let the player and Megabonk's\n  spawn raycasts fall through (fixed in 0.1.9).\n- **The current map index (5) is unnamed** [14]: one group per region numbered `x << 8 | y`, file 0 terrain (16-bit\n  attributes, a trailing byte), file 1 objects, readable without XTEA keys. Older caches name them `m{x}_{y}`/`l{x}_{y}`.\n- **The Barrows brothers' NPC models are pink in the cache** [22]: hue 60 on every face, recoloured by the client at\n  run time; their armour items are grey. `enemies.model_ids` `worn:<item ids>` builds Dharok and\n  Ahrim as a male body in their own armour instead (`Osrs.BuildWorn`); `bonk modelcolors <model...>` prints a\n  model's commonest face colours.\n- **Some places are on another level.** The God Wars Dungeon's cavern and three of its rooms are on level 2, and Zilyana's\n  room is on level 0. Built as level 0, the God Wars box had 415 walkable tiles of 16,384. `maps.osrs_plane` and\n  `bosses.arena_plane` (0.11) name the level: `WorldBuilder` works out every level's heights as usual, then shifts\n  that level down to be the ground. It also leaves out objects below it and turns the raised-house bridge logic off. `bonk world`, `tiles` and `boxes`\n  take `plane=N`, and so do the preflight's tile checks. Every caller must pass the level: `Osrs`'s load-time build of\n  every map left it out at first, so God Wars came out as level 0's empty box (the adventurer kept falling through)\n  whenever that build reached it before a run on it did. The build's log line names the level (`on level 2`).\n- **Textures are one average colour.** Faces with a texture are drawn in its sprite's average colour, part of why\n  Lumbridge looks paler than in OSRS.\n\n- **Megabonk renders in Linear colour space** [21]. OSRS colours are gamma (sRGB) values; as vertex colours in a Linear\n  project they come out paler. `UnityConv` converts each channel gamma-to-linear when\n  `QualitySettings.activeColorSpace` is Linear (logged as *\"colours: Linear colour space, ...\"*).\n\n## Sizing and the arena\n\n- **Measure the character once, upright** [18]. Player collider bounds read 0 (or tilted) at some moments. The\n  height is kept for the game session while the adventurer stays the same; since 0.1.30 `Sizing.Measure` resets and\n  measures again when the adventurer changes (`sizing: adventurer changed (A -> B), measuring again` in the log), so an\n  adventurer whose Megabonk character has a different collider gets its own measurement.\n- **Arena colliders** [17]: walls are separate meshes in 8x8-tile cells, hidden while between camera and adventurer.\n  The colliders sit on a free physics layer (the highest unnamed one; layer 0 until 0.1.11) that is set to collide\n  with the player's and the enemy layer, while Megabonk's camera, spawn raycasts and projectiles ignore it.\n- **Monster hitboxes read 0 at spawn, then turn out huge.** `Enemy.collider` bounds are 0 when `InitEnemy` runs and\n  go live a moment later, which is also when Megabonk enlarges elites and minibosses; `FitHitbox` re-sizes then.\n  Megabonk's hitboxes are tall capsules (5.4 m for a 3 m skeleton), so \"never smaller than the hitbox\" made monsters\n  far too big: the floor is off by default since 0.1.15 (`Models.MinHitboxShare`).\n- **Megabonk's camera isn't always `Camera.main`.** The wall hiding looks for `PlayerCamera`'s own camera; the minimap\n  has a separate orthographic camera (whose `orthographicSize` `Minimap.Zoom` divides).\n- **A Megabonk map's stages share one `EMap`**: Bonkscape tells them apart by the stage loaded (`Bonk.StageIndex`), so\n  each stage carries its own `maps` row (`bonk_base_stage`); before 0.5 every stage of the Forest got the same\n  Lumbridge.\n\n## Build, release and docs\n\n- **`tools/package.py` builds the plugin itself.** Before, it zipped whatever was in `bin/Release`: 0.2.1's\n  first zip carried a DLL built before the last three edits (its log said 0.2.0). It now runs `dotnet build -c Release`\n  first and refuses when `Plugin.Version` isn't the version asked for.\n- **Renaming a config key is how you reset a default.** BepInEx keeps the value already in a player's `.cfg`, so a\n  new default never reaches existing installs. `HitboxFill` became `MinHitboxShare` in 0.1.15 for that reason (and\n  `PlayerFit` became `AdventurerScale` in 0.1.12, with a new default); the old lines are then ignored.\n- **Bump `Plugin.Version` for every upload**; the version Melty shows comes from the release, the log line from the DLL.\n- **The DLL records the commit it was built from**: its informational version is `1.0.0+<HEAD hash>`. Uncommitted\n  changes aren't reflected, so a zip built from a dirty tree carries the previous commit's hash (0.1.0 to 0.1.3 all\n  say `7b77aa6`).\n- **`tools/package.py` copies `README.md` as it is.** Update the README before packaging: 0.1.7 to 0.1.11 shipped the\n  pre-arena README describing \"Megabonk's Forest map becomes Lumbridge\".\n- **The preflight writes generated code even when it has phase-1 findings.** Check its exit code (4 = open items).\n- **Give every new patch its `sheets/hooks.json` row.** The preflight checks that each row's target still exists in\n  Megabonk's code, so a patch without a row goes unchecked; and nothing compares the sheet with the code (`check_docs`\n  compares the code with [Architecture's patch table](architecture.md#harmony-patches) only). Until 0.15 (`9a86133`)\n  six patches had no row, seven rows gave the wrong method or patch kind, and one (`timer`) matched no patch.\n- **Starting weapons are set in the `DataManager.Load` postfix**, once per game start; the character select panel\n  computes them live, so it can show a weapon the run doesn't give until Megabonk restarts (untested).\n\n<!-- sources: docs/MODLOG.md -->\n"}, {"section": "dev", "slug": "hub", "text": "# The hub\n\nOne local page that links every Bonkscape web tool and starts, stops and restarts their servers, so nothing has to be\nstarted by hand. It runs on this PC only, at <http://localhost:8789>.\n\n## Starting it\n\n- **Desktop:** double-click **Bonkscape Hub**. It runs `pythonw`, so no console window opens. Make the shortcut once\n  with `python tools/hub/hub.py --shortcut` (or the button at the bottom of the hub).\n- **Repo:** double-click `Bonkscape Hub.cmd` in the repo root (a console flashes and closes).\n- **Terminal:** `python tools/hub/hub.py --open`.\n- **Claude Code:** the `bonkscape-hub` entry in `.claude/launch.json` (serves the hub without opening a browser).\n\nStarting the hub starts the wiki and the map sketch tool when they aren't running, then opens the hub in your browser.\nLaunching it again while it runs just opens the page. When `hub.py` or `hub.json` changed since the running hub\nstarted, the new hub replaces it; the servers keep running. `python tools/hub/hub.py --stop-all` stops the hub and\nevery server.\n\n## What's on it\n\n| Section | What | Port |\n|---|---|---|\n| BSWiki | `tools/wiki-site/server.py`: `docs/wiki` and `docs/dev`, with links to the player home, getting started, the dev home, changelog, docket, gotchas and this page | 8791 |\n| Map sketch tool | `tools/sketch/server.py`: the world map from your cache, your outlines and the Ideas layer, plus a link to the map ideas write-up | 8790 |\n| Docs | Markdown the wiki site doesn't show, rendered by the hub's own viewer (`doc.html`): the README, `docs/design/` (map ideas, UI style), `docs/MODLOG.md`, `docs/preflight.md`, and osrs-kit's README, `docs/`, `docs/research/` and `tools/music/README.md` | 8789 |\n| Folders | Opens the repo, `sketches/`, `dist/`, osrs-kit or the hub's logs in Explorer | |\n| Commands | The docs checker, table generator, map ideas build, packaging, the wiki export, the offline OSRS Wiki, the music lookup and `osrs.exe gamevals`, with absolute paths and a Copy button | |\n\nEach server card shows whether it's running, its port and pid, who started it (the hub, or something else such as\nClaude Code's preview) and since when, with Start, Stop, Restart and Log buttons. **Start all**, **Stop all** and\n**Quit** (leaving the servers running, or stopping them too) are at the top.\n\n## How it handles servers\n\n- **Reuse.** The hub recognises a server by what it serves (the wiki by `/api/nav`, the sketch tool by its `app.js`),\n  not just by a busy port, so a server started elsewhere (Claude Code's preview, a terminal) is reused, never doubled.\n- **Free ports.** When another program holds a server's port, Start takes the first free port from the default + 100\n  (8890, 8891, ... for the sketch tool) and remembers it; the card links there. The defaults stay 8789 to 8791.\n- **No windows.** Servers run under `python.exe` with no window (`CREATE_NO_WINDOW`), their output going to a log.\n  They keep running when the hub quits or crashes, and the next hub finds them.\n- **Older code.** When a running server's `server.py` changed after the server started, its card says so, and Restart\n  loads the new code. The hub never restarts a server by itself.\n- **Stop** ends the process that holds the port (found with `netstat -ano`), also one the hub didn't start; the page\n  asks first in that case.\n\n## Logs and state\n\nOutside the repo, in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bonkscape\\hub\\`:\n\n- `logs\\<server>.log`: each server's output (the card's Log button shows the end), trimmed past 1 MB.\n- `logs\\hub.log`: the hub's own output when it runs without a console.\n- `state.json`: the hub's port, and each server's last port and pid.\n- `config.txt`: the hub config that owns this folder; another project with the same name gets\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bonkscape-<6 hex>\\hub\\` instead.\n\nThe engine also lists this hub in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\project-hub\\hubs.json`, the PC-wide registry the kit's\n`scaffold.py` reads to give a new project ports no other hub uses.\n- `hub.ico`: the shortcut's icon, drawn by `hub.py` (no image file in the repo).\n\nIf double-clicking does nothing, read `hub.log`, or run `python tools/hub/hub.py --open` in a terminal to see the error.\n\n## Safety\n\n- It listens on 127.0.0.1 only. It refuses requests whose `Host` isn't `localhost` or `127.0.0.1` (DNS rebinding),\n  and buttons that change anything send an `X-Project-Hub` header that another website can't send to it; a foreign\n  `Origin` is refused too. So no web page can start or stop anything.\n- The doc viewer serves only the files `hub.json`'s doc groups list; the folder buttons open only its `folders`.\n- Stdlib Python, nothing to install. Made for Windows: elsewhere pids come from `lsof`, and there's no shortcut and no\n  older-code check.\n\n## Adding a tool\n\nEverything Bonkscape-specific is in `tools/hub/hub.json`: `services` (a server: id, name, port, the command with\n`{port}`, a `probe` that only it answers, links), `docs` (Markdown the hub renders, and `routes` for pages the wiki\nrenders), `folders` and `commands`. The full list of keys is in the project-hub kit's `config.md`. Then:\n\n```\npython tools/hub/hub.py --check       validates hub.json\npython tools/hub/hub.py --selftest    serves the hub on a spare port and fetches every page and doc\n```\n\nRelaunch the hub (it replaces the running one, because hub.json changed), then add the tool to the table above and to\n[Architecture](architecture.md#projects).\n\n**The engine** (`hub.py`, `index.html`, `app.js`, `style.css`, `doc.html`, `doc.js`) is a copy from the project-hub\nkit, its own repo beside this one, which any project can use. Don't edit it here: change the kit, then copy it in with\n`python ../project-hub/scaffold.py update .` from the repo root. `export_wiki.py` is Bonkscape's own.\n\n## Hosting BSWiki\n\nBSWiki is online, **public**, at <https://jacksonarnold27.github.io/bswiki/>: the player and developer pages, from the\npublic repo [jacksonarnold27/bswiki](https://github.com/jacksonarnold27/bswiki) (GitHub Pages, branch `main`). That repo\nholds only the exported wiki; Bonkscape's own repo stays private. Its local clone sits beside this repo, as `bswiki`\n(`hub.json`'s `site`, `{root}/../bswiki`), and its commits use the GitHub no-reply address, not your e-mail.\n\n**Who updates it, and how:** the main Bonkscape session, after each release's wiki-update is committed. It exports\nfrom a clean worktree of HEAD, so other sessions' unfinished edits in the shared checkout never go public:\n\n1. `git worktree add --detach <scratch folder>/bsw-head HEAD`\n2. In that worktree: `python -I tools/hub/export_wiki.py --with-dev --out <the bswiki clone> --push`, naming the\n   clone by its full path (`../bswiki` would point beside the worktree, not beside this repo).\n3. `git worktree remove <scratch folder>/bsw-head`\n\nThe export commits in the clone and pushes; the site changes a minute or two later, and nothing is pushed when the\nexport hasn't changed. The hub's Commands card has the short form (`export_wiki.py --with-dev --out {site}\n--push`, `{site}` being the clone beside this repo): it exports this checkout's working tree as it is, other sessions'\nedits included, so use it only when nobody else is editing the docs.\n\n**What the export holds:** the wiki site's `index.html`, `app.js` (its paths made relative, so it works in a\nsubfolder) and `style.css`, the Markdown pages, `api/nav` and `api/pages` (what the local server's API returns), plus\n`robots.txt`, a `_headers` file (for Cloudflare Pages) and a `noindex` tag asking search engines not to list it, and\n`.nojekyll` (so GitHub Pages serves the `.md` files as they are). It saves what the wiki server serves, so it's the\nlocal site page for page. Without `--with-dev` it's the player pages only; `--serve` shows an export on\n<http://localhost:8799>; with no `--out` it goes to `build/bswiki-site/` (gitignored).\n\n**Never online:** map tiles, sprites, models, renders or anything else read from the OSRS cache or Megabonk. The\nexport is text only and refuses a page that embeds an image or any other file. Anything written in `docs/wiki` or\n`docs/dev` goes public with the next update, so keep personal paths and private notes out of those pages (the docs\ncheck's [Paths](updating-docs.md#the-docs-checker) rule fails on a path from this PC).\n\nFor a private copy instead, Cloudflare Pages can serve the same export\n(`npx wrangler pages deploy build/bswiki-site --project-name bswiki`) behind a Cloudflare Access application (Zero\nTrust, free for up to 50 users) that lets in only your e-mail address. GitHub Pages sites on a free account are\nalways public.\n\n<!-- sources: tools/hub/ -->\n"}, {"section": "dev", "slug": "index", "text": "# BSWiki: developer pages\n\nHow Bonkscape is built, why it is the way it is, and what's next. Player-facing pages are in [BSWiki's player pages](../wiki/index.md).\nWritten against **0.16.1** (code at `f716b88`).\n\n| Page | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| [Architecture](architecture.md) | Projects, load sequence, the background asset build, sheets to code, the run lifecycle, the arena pipeline, every Harmony patch, tools, build and release |\n| [Sheets reference](sheets-reference.md) | Every sheet and column, what reads it, and what the preflight checks |\n| [Gotchas](gotchas.md) | Hard-won lessons from `MODLOG.md`, grouped by topic |\n| [Testing](testing.md) | The local loop and its scenarios, Melty test mode, self-checks, footage review, the test history |\n| [Cookbook](cookbook.md) | Recipes: add a map, a boss arena, a weapon and its special attack, an adventurer, a test check |\n| [Changelog and patch notes](changelog.md) | Every version 0.0.1 to 0.16.1: date, commit, test, patch notes and changes |\n| [Docket](docket.md) | Ideas and tasks by milestone, with status |\n| [How to update these docs](updating-docs.md) | What to update with every version, the table generator and the checker |\n| [The hub](hub.md) | One page that starts and links the wiki, the map sketch tool and the docs (`Bonkscape Hub.cmd`); the public BSWiki at jacksonarnold27.github.io/bswiki |\n\n## Where things are\n\n```\nsheets/*.json                      design data (source of truth)\n../osrs-kit/src/OsrsKit.Cache/     OSRS cache reader (no Unity), in osrs-kit beside this repo\nsrc/Bonkscape.Plugin/              the BepInEx plugin\n  Core/                            runner, asset build, sizing, Unity conversion, rows, JSON\n  Dev/                             data dump, timing marks, test breadcrumbs\n  Systems/                         one file per system (TitleArt.cs and HudLayout.cs are Unity-free, also in bonk)\n  Patches/CorePatches.cs           leaderboards, data load, death screen\n  Generated/Sheets.g.cs            generated from the sheets, don't edit\nsrc/Bonkscape.Tools/               bonk.exe (preflight, codegen, cache and map tools)\ntools/package.py                   release zip (and its gates)\ntools/loop.py, tools/scenarios/    the local loop and its scenarios (Testing)\ntools/pipeline/                    a map's, lair's or adventurer's rows and checks, staged from an outline\ntools/mapideas/                    map ideas and monster rosters (writes docs/design/)\ntools/sketch/                      map sketch tool (port 8790)\ntools/wiki-site/                   this wiki's site (port 8791), table generator, docs checker\ntools/hub/                         the hub (port 8789): starts and links the tools; Bonkscape Hub.cmd launches it\ndocs/MODLOG.md                     short log: versions, workflow, numbered gotchas\ndocs/preflight.md                  generated preflight report\ndocs/design/                       milestone plan, map ideas, UI style, private reference (not in BSWiki)\ndocs/wiki/, docs/dev/              these wikis\n```\n\n## The working loop\n\n1. Change the sheets (design) or the code.\n2. `bonk preflight ...` until phase 1 has 0 open items (regenerates `Sheets.g.cs` and `cache_assets.json`).\n3. Try it with the local loop (`python -I tools/loop.py tools/scenarios/<name>.json`): it builds, installs, plays a\n   scenario and reports ([Testing](testing.md#the-local-loop)).\n4. Once per release: bump `Plugin.Version`, `python tools/package.py <version>` (it builds the plugin first), upload\n   and test through Melty; read the self-checks and review the footage ([Testing](testing.md)).\n5. Update the docs: changelog and patch notes, affected wiki pages, docket, statuses\n   ([How to update these docs](updating-docs.md)).\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Plugin.cs -->\n"}, {"section": "dev", "slug": "sheets-reference", "text": "# Sheets reference\n\n`sheets/*.json` is the design data and the source of truth. Every system starts as rows here; the code reads them\nthrough `Generated/Sheets.g.cs`. Each file has the same shape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sheet\": \"prayers\",\n  \"about\": \"One line on what a row is\",\n  \"columns\": [\"id\", \"name\", \"...\", \"phase\", \"status\"],\n  \"rows\": [ { \"id\": \"protect_melee\", \"name\": \"Protect from Melee\", \"...\": \"...\", \"phase\": 1, \"status\": \"verified\" } ]\n}\n```\n\n<!-- gen:sheet-list -->\n| Sheet | Rows | Columns | Row statuses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `audio.json` | 14 | 6 | 11 built, 3 todo |\n| `bosses.json` | 9 | 22 | 8 built, 1 verified |\n| `cache_assets.json` | 686 | 9 | 7 todo, 679 verified |\n| `characters.json` | 14 | 22 | 9 built, 5 verified |\n| `clue_steps.json` | 42 | 9 | 42 built |\n| `drops.json` | 35 | 9 | 32 built, 1 todo, 2 verified |\n| `enemies.json` | 116 | 22 | 96 built, 2 todo, 18 verified |\n| `evolutions.json` | 4 | 10 | 3 built, 1 todo |\n| `hooks.json` | 52 | 8 | 23 built, 29 verified |\n| `maps.json` | 7 | 24 | 5 built, 2 verified |\n| `passives.json` | 11 | 11 | 11 built |\n| `prayers.json` | 3 | 12 | 3 verified |\n| `skills.json` | 10 | 8 | 5 built, 5 verified |\n| `ui.json` | 41 | 7 | 4 built, 1 todo, 36 verified |\n| `waves.json` | 63 | 8 | 55 built, 8 verified |\n| `weapons.json` | 34 | 29 | 32 built, 1 todo, 1 verified |\n<!-- /gen:sheet-list -->\n\n## Rules the preflight checks on every sheet\n\n`bonk preflight` (`src/Bonkscape.Tools/Preflight.cs`) loads every sheet except `cache_assets.json` and checks:\n\n- **Every row has every column**, and no row has a column the sheet doesn't declare.\n- **No cell is unfilled**: `null`, `\"\"` and `\"todo-value\"` are findings. Use `\"none\"` or `[]` for \"nothing\".\n- **`status`** is `todo`, `built` or `verified`.\n- **`phase`** sorts the findings: phase-1 findings are \"open items\" and make the preflight exit with code 4; phase 2/3\n  findings are listed as \"unfinished by design\". The report and the generated code are written either way, so read\n  the exit code.\n- **References** to other sheets resolve (`'goblin' is not a row of enemies`), with `none`/`any` allowed where noted,\n  for the columns whose Preflight cell below says so. A column whose cell says nothing isn't checked: a typo there\n  passes the preflight and shows only in game.\n- **Megabonk names** resolve against `Assembly-CSharp.dll` in the interop folder: enum values (`EWeapon`, `ETome`,\n  `EEnemy`, `ECharacter`, `EMap`, `EStat`, `EStatModifyType`; a `new:` prefix skips the check), and for `hooks`, classes\n  and methods.\n- **OSRS ids** resolve in the dev cache: definitions decode and their models load (NPCs, items, objects, kits, worn\n  items, spot animations), sequences are frame-based (not skeletal) so they bake, sprites and fonts exist, sounds\n  synthesise to something. Each asset becomes a row of\n  `cache_assets.json`, also checked against this PC's own (player) cache.\n\nThe report goes to `docs/preflight.md`, `cache_assets.json` is rewritten, and the generated rows go to\n`Generated/Sheets.g.cs` when a `genOut` path is given (pass `-` to skip).\n\nIn the tables below, the Preflight column says what the preflight checks in that column beyond these rules; a blank\ncell means nothing more.\n\n### Status, by convention\n\n| Status | Means |\n|---|---|\n| `todo` | Designed, not built. The plugin ignores rows with `phase` > 1 whatever their status. |\n| `built` | In the code. |\n| `verified` | Seen working in a Melty test run (self-check or footage). |\n\nThe preflight only checks the spelling, and no plugin code reads a row's status (only `phase`): it's for the docs and\nthe wiki's tables. Keeping statuses honest is a manual step at each release ([How to update these docs](updating-docs.md)).\n\n## characters\n\nOne row per playable adventurer; each takes over a Megabonk character. Read by `Content.ApplyCharacter`,\n`Osrs.BuildPlayer`, `PlayerSkin`, `Combat.CurrentCharacter`, `TestMode` (autoplay takes the unlocked rows in turn).\n\n| Column | Meaning | Preflight |\n|---|---|---|\n| `id` | Row key. | |\n| `name` | Shown name (character select, `GetName`). | |\n| `style` | `melee` / `ranged` / `magic`. Decides which skill the default damage trains and which weapons can be its starting weapon. | |\n| `attack_style` | The style a new adventurer starts on (0.14; the style key and the combat tab change it, saved per adventurer in `unlocks.json` as `_style_<id>`, `Systems/AttackStyles.cs`): `accurate`, `aggressive`, `controlled` or `defensive`. With melee damage they train Attack, Strength, all three, Defence; with ranged, Ranged (`aggressive` is Rapid) or Ranged and Defence (`defensive` is Longrange); with magic, Magic or Magic and Defence (`defensive` is defensive casting). `Skills.Shares` holds the split. | one of the four; not one that trains a locked skill |\n| `starting_weapon` | 0.14: a ladder's first tier (`bronze_scimitar`, `shortbow`, `wind_strike`) makes the adventurer climb that ladder: they start with its highest tier their own levels allow, or a weapon of their style beyond its top once they meet it (`Content.StartingWeapon`). Any other weapon is the adventurer's own, given whatever their levels (the Zerker's dragon scimitar, Durial321's whip). | row of `weapons` |\n| `bonk_base_character` | Megabonk `ECharacter` whose slot, prefab, movement and hitbox it takes. | `ECharacter` value |\n| `hp`, `armor`, `damage`, `area`, `cooldown`, `speed`, `luck` | Stat modifiers replacing the Megabonk character's own for `MaxHealth`, `Armor`, `DamageMultiplier`, `SizeMultiplier`, `AttackSpeed` (flat) and `MoveSpeedMultiplier` (addition), `Luck` (flat). 0 = remove Megabonk's. | |\n| `osrs_player_model` | JSON: `osrs_items` (worn item ids), `osrs_kits_male` (identity kit ids), `osrs_colors` (0 = kit colours; not read, the kits' own colours are always used), `osrs_stand_anim` (sequence for the pose), `osrs_walk_anim` (819), `osrs_run_anim` (824) and `osrs_death_anim` (836) for animations (0.2.0). | every kit and worn item resolves; the whole body builds |\n| `can_buy` | `false`: the Shady Guy, and every map's shopkeeper standing in for him (TzHaar-Hur-Tel), refuse this adventurer (the Ironman; `Loot`'s Interact patch, \"Ironmen can't trade\" on the prompt). | |\n| `extra_weapons` | JSON list of weapons ids given beside the starting weapon once a run's inventory exists, their `gear_reqs` not asked (`Content.GiveExtraWeapons`): Durial321's `[\"ice_barrage\"]`. `[]` for none. | rows of `weapons` |\n| `unlock_condition` | Replaces Megabonk's unlock for the base character the row takes over, either way (`Unlocks.CharacterUnlocked`, 0.6): `always`; `total_level_N`: any one adventurer has a total level of N (Ironman, 250); `barrows_set_<id>` (0.7); `levels:attack>=60,defence<=1`: one played adventurer's levels meet every part (0.14, the Pure). | one of those forms |\n| `locked_skills` | JSON list of skills the adventurer never trains (0.14: the Pure's `[\"defence\"]`): their XP is dropped (`Skills.Add`), and styles that would train one are never offered. `[]` for none. | skills ids |\n| `start_levels` | JSON `{skill: level}`: an adventurer with no skills save of their own starts at these levels (0.7: a Barrows brother at 70 in Attack, Strength, Defence, Hitpoints and his style's skill; `Skills.Of`, not saved until he earns XP). `{}` for none. | rows of `skills`; levels 1 to 99 |\n| `set_effect` | JSON: the adventurer's Barrows set effect (0.7), the same as his `enemies` row's: `name` (`dharok_rage`, `ahrim_weaken`, `guthan_heal`, `karil_slow`, `torag_drain`, `verac_pierce`), `chance` (0.25; Dharok's 1.0), the style it triggers on (`on`), its numbers and `spotanim`. `{}` for none. | |\n| `phase`, `status` | See above. | |\n\n## weapons\n\nOne row per OSRS weapon; each reuses a Megabonk weapon's behaviour. Read by `Content.ApplyWeapon`, `Combat`,\n`Content.StartingWeapon`, `Content.ApplyLadders`, `Osrs` (items, sprites, sounds).\n\n| Column | Meaning | Preflight |\n|---|---|---|\n| `id`, `name` | Key and shown name. | |\n| `style` | `melee` / `ranged` / `magic`: which skill its damage trains, and which adventurers can start with it. | |\n| `bonk_base` | Megabonk `EWeapon` it takes over, or `new:...` for a weapon type that doesn't exist yet (the plugin can't make one: such a row isn't built). Several rows may share a type when all but one are character-only: the other row is the type's default, and a character-only one goes on it only while its owner is chosen or plays (0.7: Guthan's, Torag's and Verac's weapons on CorruptSword, the Void knight mace's; `Content.ApplyCharacterWeapons`). | `EWeapon` value |\n| `damage`, `cooldown`, `area`, `amount`, `speed`, `pierce`, `duration`, `knockback` | Replace `WeaponData.damage`, `endCooldown`, `projectiles`, `projectileSpeed`, `projectileBounces` (from `pierce`), `attackDuration`, `knockback`, and the `baseStats` entries `DamageMultiplier`, `SizeMultiplier` (area), `Projectiles`, `ProjectileSpeedMultiplier`, `ProjectileBounces`, `DurationMultiplier`, `KnockbackMultiplier`. | |\n| `level_curve` | List of `Stat:Type:Value` that replaces the weapon's upgrade pool. | each `EStat` and `EStatModifyType` |\n| `gear_reqs` | `{\"skill\": level}`: OSRS's levels to wield it (0.14, from the OSRS Wiki): level-ups never offer it to an adventurer below them (`Content.Offered`: their own starting and extra weapons, and any weapon held, ask nothing), and it's never a starting weapon below them. Or `\"character:<id>\"` (0.7: a Barrows brother's own weapon: only he starts with it, it's never upgraded away from him, and level-up offers give it to him alone). | skills are rows of `skills`; each level listed in `skills.unlocks` |\n| `ladder`, `tier` | 0.14: rows sharing a ladder (`scimitar`: bronze 1 .. dragon 60 Attack; `shortbow`: normal 1 .. magic 50 Ranged; `strike`: Wind 1, Water 5, Earth 9, Fire 13 Magic) share one Megabonk weapon and its special attack; the adventurer playing gets the highest tier they meet, a tier asking the same level as the one below being climbed one level later (`Rows.ClimbLevel`: bronze and iron scimitars both ask Attack 1, so a new adventurer starts with bronze and gets iron at Attack 2) (`Content.ApplyLadders`, as character select's choice changes and as a run starts; a level-up mid-run says it comes with the next run), each tier with its 