n 1 the Ranger on Lumbridge: a clue, the willow shortbow's combat tab, the Abyssal tentacle, the Giant Mole's lair. Run 2 on the Wilderness: green dragons and wolves, a clue, the dragon scimitar's tab, the tentacle, the boss door into the King Black Dragon's lair after a final-stage load of about 10 s. Run 3 on Al Kharid in Ahrim's robes: Ice Barrage, a clue, the whip's tab, a Charge Shrine window, the Kalphite Queen's lair; Megabonk's own main menu showed for one frame before the title screen. All 12 each. |\n| 0.15.2 | `a348ef8e` | 3 x 2 min | **passed (publish test)** | Run 1 on Morytania with the Void knight mace: a clue, the combat tab, the Abyssal tentacle, Torag as the hidden brother. Run 2 on Ardougne & Seers' Village with an iron scimitar: a clue, the combat tab, the tentacle, Sir Lancelot's arena. Run 3 on the God Wars Dungeon with Fire Strike: a clue, the combat tab, the level-up offers in OSRS's stone, the tentacle, Kree'arra's lair after the final-stage load. All 12 each. |\n| 0.16.0 | `b4021c47` | 3 x 2 min (1 run) | **failed** | Run 1 on TzHaar City passed all 12 self-checks, but `loader-errors` failed on one logged warning, *lava: NullReferenceException*, as the final portal loaded the Fight Caves (`LavaBurn` read the adventurer Megabonk had just destroyed). The test stopped after run 1. |\n| 0.16.1 | `ee096614` | 3 x 2 min | **passed (publish test)** | Run 1 on Lumbridge with Fire Strike: a clue, its casting styles, the Abyssal tentacle, the Giant Mole's lair. Run 2 on the Wilderness with the Void knight mace: a clue, its styles, the tentacle, the King Black Dragon's lair. Run 3 on Al Kharid with an iron scimitar: a clue, its styles, the tentacle, the Kalphite Queen's lair. All 12 each. Melty's runs press no controller buttons; the `controller-pad` scenario does, through its `pad` steps. |\n\nBlank cells weren't looked up. `mcp__melty__test_result` with the test id gives the full record.\n\n## The local loop\n\n`python -I tools/loop.py tools/scenarios/<name>.json [more.json ...] [--one-launch] [--no-build] [--shots N]`, or a\nscenario made from flags instead of a file: `--map`, `--stage`, `--adventurer`, `--seconds`, `--boss-at`,\n`--step at:kind:arg` (`--step 5:spawn:giant_mole`), `--level skill=N`, `--expect <check>`, `--no-autoplay`; with\n`--width`, `--height`, `--timeout` and `--hang-after` (`loop.py`'s own usage text has examples):\n\n1. Refuses if a Megabonk is running (it never closes a game it didn't start) or Melty's test file exists.\n2. Builds the plugin (`dotnet build`, about 1.5 s when little changed), backs up `BepInEx/plugins/Bonkscape`, installs\n   `Bonkscape.dll`, `OsrsKit.Cache.dll` and the local-only music engine (an older install's `Bonkscape.Cache.dll` is\n   set aside until the run ends), switches Melty's loader on\n   (`winhttp.dll.melty-off` to `winhttp.dll`) and writes the scenario to `BepInEx/config/Bonkscape/scenario.json`.\n3. Backs up every save a run writes (Megabonk's `Saves` folder, which Steam Cloud syncs, and its Player logs;\n   Bonkscape's skill saves, unlocks, breadcrumbs and test rotation) and launches `Megabonk.exe` directly (no Steam hand-off on\n   this PC). Claude's app package doesn't isolate them: it redirects only new AppData files, and a run overwrites\n   existing saves ([Gotchas](gotchas.md#megabonk-runtime)). Scenarios also turn skill saving off unless they test it\n   (`\"save_skills\": true`). Megabonk ignores Unity's `-screen-*` flags and runs at its own settings.\n4. Waits for the scenario report's last line, `done:`, then copies `LogOutput.log`, `scenario-report.txt` and the\n   screenshots to `derived/loop/<time>-<name>/` (gitignored), closes the PID it started, and puts the plugin folder,\n   the loader switch and every save back, byte for byte with their times (files the run made are removed). A loop\n   killed before that restores the saves on its next start (`derived/loop/PENDING-RESTORE.txt`). A hang ends the wait\n   early: after `--hang-after` seconds (90) without log output, with the game's window not responding (asked at most every 10 s), the loop writes\n   `hang-threads.txt` (each thread's state, CPU time and name) and `hang.dmp` (a minidump with every thread's stack,\n   for WinDbg; `tools/hang_report.ps1`, stopped after 180 s) into that folder before it closes the game ([Docket](docket.md): local suites\n   hang at a scene load).\n5. Prints a summary: timings, the in-game timing marks (`timing: <mark> at N s` lines from `Dev/Timing.cs`), the\n   checks that passed, every Bonkscape warning or error, the dead-hook report, the game's memory at each scenario's\n   end, the hang if there was one, and the scenario report. Several scenario files run one after another with a\n   combined table; `--one-launch` runs them as a suite in one game: each row counts only its own scenario's warnings and\n   errors, and the scenarios a hang or a timeout left unfinished or unrun (all of them when the suite never got\n   going) are failures in its table.\n\n**Scenarios** (`Systems/Scenario.cs`, files in `tools/scenarios/`): `map`, `stage` (a later stage is reached from the\nfirst, `TestMode.JumpToStage`; the clock waits for it, and its monsters lose Megabonk's per-stage hp and damage share, as\nthe adventurer arrives without the levels a real run brings: [Gotchas](gotchas.md)), `adventurer`, `autoplay`,\n`boss_at` (seconds: the boss portal is opened, or a map's last stage's final portal, tried each second until one\nexists), `arena_time_scale` (a boss arena's wave times and `boss_at` multiplied by it; Melty's autoplay runs use 0.25\nand call the boss at 40 s on a map whose boss has `arena_waves`, 75 s otherwise, so the Fight Caves' waves and Jad fit\na two-minute run), `seconds`, `steps` (`{\"at\": 3, \"spawn\": \"giant_mole\"}` spawns a monster 4 tiles ahead\n(`\"goblin:1\"`: one tile ahead), or the stage boss\nthrough `EnemyManager.SpawnBoss`; `\"weapon\": \"<weapons id>\"` gives one; `\"drop\": \"prayer_potion\"` or `\"casket\"`;\n`\"kill\": \"boss\"` ends the live stage or final boss through Megabonk's own death call, so what a boss's death unlocks runs\n(the Fire cape after Jad; a test adventurer can't kill a third-stage boss in a scenario's time); `\"shop\": \"interact\"` puts the adventurer by the nearest Shady Guy (or the shopkeeper standing in for him) and\npresses Interact, reporting whether he traded (`shop-refused` for an Ironman); `\"lava\": \"edge\"` puts\nthe adventurer against the nearest lava edge with a burn due at once (autoplay never walks into one: lava has colliders);\n`\"probe\": \"<regex>\"` logs the active scene objects whose type matches (`\"text:<regex>\"`: the on-screen texts) and `\"interact\": \"<type>\"` presses the nearest interactable of that type (0.9's study of the Graveyard; a scenario on a map not built yet plays Megabonk's own map under it and runs only its steps and shots); `\"teleport\": \"x,y,z\"` moves the adventurer; `\"pause\": \"on\"` or `\"off\"` sets Megabonk's time scale as its level-up and reward windows do (the scenario clock stops with it, so a step meant under the pause shares its time); `\"gold\": \"12345\"` adds that much gold (through Megabonk's `ChangeGold` up to its 10,000 a frame, straight into the purse above that); `\"tab\": \"prayer\"`, `\"inventory\"` or `\"combat\"` opens that side panel tab, as F5 or a click does; `\"style\": \"next\"` presses the style key (and shows the combat tab), `\"style\": \"defensive\"` (or another style) picks it, refused when it isn't one of the adventurer's (the Pure's); `\"xp\": \"attack,strength,defence\"` reports those skills' XP; `\"pad\": \"<button>\"` presses that controller button (a\n`[Controller]` button name) for the next frame, as a pad would (`Controls.Inject`); `\"clue\": \"drop\"` makes the next kill drop a clue scroll, `\"start\"` starts a trail at once, `\"solve\"` moves the adventurer to the current step and does it, `\"near\"` moves them five tiles from it (for the hint arrow's screenshot); `\"pet\": \"drop\"` makes the next boss death with a pet drop it, `\"pet\": \"give:<bosses id>\"` makes that pet owned and out; `\"pause_menu\": \"open\"` or `\"close\"` opens or closes Megabonk's pause menu (through its `PauseHandler`); `\"interact\"` takes a type name ending in `*` for any of that kind (`InteractableShrine*`); `\"spec\": \"<weapons id>\"` arms that held weapon's special attack, fills the bar and fires it (with nothing in reach, it stays armed and fires at the first monster in reach within\n4 s, autoplay leaving the specs alone meanwhile), `\"spec\": \"switch\"` presses\nthe switch key; `\"camera\": \"far\"`, `\"near\"` or `\"north\"` sets the camera; `\"evolve\": \"<evolutions id>\"` readies an evolution (the weapon levelled, its passive given) and does\nwhat a casket's Open button does). A spawned monster can't be hurt for about its first second (`Enemy.CanTakeDamage`),\nso a spec step comes 1.5 s after its monsters' spawn,\n`levels` (preset, never saved), `seed_levels` (`{\"warrior\": 30}`: every skill of another adventurer at that level, in\nmemory only, for unlocks that look at other adventurers: Ironman's total level 250; `{\"warrior\": {\"attack\": 60, \"defence\": 1}}`: only those skills, 0.14's Pure), `seed_barrows` (`[\"dharok\"]`: that\nbrother's full Barrows set owned, in memory only, for his unlock), `pool_boss` (`\"kril_tsutsaroth\"`: that member of the map's `boss_pool` is the run's boss, not a random one), `reload_saves` (`true`: unlocks read again from\nunlocks.json, so a suite's later scenario proves a piece the earlier one saved survives a restart: `barrows-save` then\n`barrows-load`; the report counts the pieces owned and says whether the\nplaying adventurer is unlocked), `save_skills` (`true` to test saving; the loop restores the files after), `shots` (seconds), `expect` (checks the report marks `pass` or `MISSING`), `deaths`\n(`\"allow\"` for real deaths), `ui` (the UI test; its `menus` may include `\"title\"` (0.12): at the last resolution each title screen button a test may press is pressed, what it opened screenshotted and the menu brought back, and Exit, Language and Discord are only checked as wired, so a test never quits, changes the language or opens a browser; a return to the main menu while it's open is skipped, since Megabonk's `SetWindow` toggles and would switch the menu off), `title_screen` (`false`: Megabonk's own main menu for that scenario), `music` (`true` for the OSRS music; it's off in scenarios otherwise, since 0.12: Melty's builds never carry the engine, and local suites hung at scene loads with it, though they have since without it too: the docket's row), `dump_logo` (`true`: Megabonk's logo as the title screen reads it back, written raw into the shots folder for `bonk uisprites mock`; Megabonk's art, kept local), `dump_input` (`true`: Megabonk's Rewired actions, map categories, joystick layouts and\ndefault gamepad map written as `<scenario>-input-dump.txt` into the shots folder, `Dev/InputDump.cs`), `suite` (several scenarios in one launch: each run is ended as at a\nrun's end and Megabonk goes back to its main menu, `TransitionUI.LoadMenu`, where the next run starts as after a launch;\nabout 5 s a scenario. Starting it straight from the run with `MapController.StartNewMap` kept the last run's player,\nits adventurer and weapons, so suites before 0.4 ran every scenario as the first one's adventurer). At a boundary\nevery adventurer's levels are read from its save again and the next scenario's `seed_levels` put back, so one\nscenario's presets and seeds never reach the next; until the next scenario's run is requested, the ending stage\nstarts no run, passes or fails no check and ticks no models or pet (`TestMode.BetweenScenarios`), and a run's end\nresets the animation counts and queued XP drops, so each scenario's `monster-animated`, `adventurer-animated` and\n`xp-drop` are its own. Each scenario\nreports `playing as <adventurer> holding <weapons>` and warns on a mismatch, and at its end `weapon attacks: <weapon>\n<count>, ...; weapon swaps N`; a suite's screenshots are collected under each scenario's own name. Something with no\nset time can ask for a screenshot (`Scenario.ShotSoon`): each weapon's first swap into the adventurer's hand gets one,\n0.3 s into the swing. The scenario's clock starts once the OSRS map is built, so\na step at 3 s never lands before the arena (a suite's later runs build theirs in 3-4 s). Scenarios start 0.3 s after\nthe main menu; Melty's runs keep 3 s. In-game screenshots (`Systems/Shot.cs`) are read back in an IMGUI repaint and\nwritten as PNG by the plugin (Unity's own capture and PNG encoder are stripped from Megabonk), halved on wide screens:\n1280x800 in about 0.25 s.\n\n| Scenario | What it checks |\n|---|---|\n| `smoke` | Morytania (the Desert's second stage since 0.8, reached by the jump) as the Mage, a whip given at 2 s, a prayer potion dropped at 4 s: `run-started`, `osrs-arena`, `osrs-enemy-killed`, `adventurer-animated` |\n| `mole-lair` | Lumbridge, the boss portal opened at 12 s: the lair swap (`boss-arena`) and a dig (`lair-dig`) |\n| `spawn-mole` | the Mole spawned straight in as the stage boss, a goblin, a blowpipe, preset levels (not saved) |\n| `ui` | the main menu, character select, map select and settings, then the run's HUD, at 1280x720, 1920x1080, 2560x1440 and 3840x2160 (about a minute) |\n| `windows`, `windows-death` | The UI test in a run (0.12): with `ui` set, each of Megabonk's windows gets a screenshot and a report line (its buttons) the first time it opens: its `Window` components (pause, chest, the Shady Guy's shop and the other encounters), the level-up offers (`LevelupScreen.isLevelingUp`) and the death screen and its stats. `windows`: a casket at 8 s, gold and the Shady Guy at 12-14 s, a shrine at 20 s, the pause menu at 26-28 s, level-ups as they come; `windows-death`: the Mage beside the Giant Mole with real deaths, for the death screen |\n| `pe