ft, the spec on Y, the prayer tab on d-pad down and back, the style on Back, the spec switch on the left stick button, the camera on the right stick button twice): `controller-action`, `prayer-switched`, `spec-fired`, `style-switched`, `combat-tab`, `spec-switched` |\n| `saves` | `\"save_skills\": true`: `Skills.Tick`'s save (every 20 s once XP changed) reads back as written, `skills-saved`; the loop restores the save after |\n| `tzhaar-city` | TzHaar City (the Desert's third stage, reached by the jump) as the Warrior: a kill, and at 15 s the adventurer put against a lava edge (`lava-burned`) |\n| `fight-caves` | TzHaar City as the Mage with Prayer, Hitpoints and Magic at 99 (not saved), the final portal at 3 s: the Fight Caves (`boss-arena`), the waves at the sheet's times (`arena-waves`), Jad released at 120 s (`arena-boss-released`) and his hits (`player-hit`) |\n| `fight-caves-quick` | the same as the Ranger at Melty's speed (`arena_time_scale` 0.25), Jad killed at 72 s by the `kill` step: `boss-blocked-fully` and `unlock-earned` |\n| `adventurer-zerker`, `adventurer-void-knight` | Lumbridge as that adventurer for 25 s: a kill and the OSRS model animated |\n| `adventurer-durial321` | the same on Morytania, and his Ice Barrage given beside the whip (`extra-weapon`) |\n| `adventurer-ironman` | Lumbridge as the Ironman, the Warrior seeded at level 30 in every skill (`seed_levels`, not saved) so he unlocks (`character-unlocked`), the Shady Guy pressed at 6 s (`shop-refused`) |\n| `morytania-barrows`, `barrows-save` | Morytania (Desert stage 1 since 0.8) as the Warrior, the boss called at 10 s and killed at about 30 s: `hidden-brother`, `boss-spawned` (the first), `barrows-piece`; `barrows-save` saves it for real |\n| `barrows-load` | `reload_saves`: the piece `barrows-save` wrote comes back from `unlocks.json` |\n| `barrows-dharok`, `barrows-guthan` | `seed_barrows`: that brother's set owned in memory, played on Lumbridge (`character-unlocked`; Guthan's warspear on its shared type, `weapon-takeover`) |\n| `barrows-sets-enemies` | the Warrior at 99 Hitpoints and Defence among spawned brothers: `player-hit`, `set-effect`, the effect counts in the report |\n| `barrows-sets-guthan`, `barrows-sets-karil` | that brother played for 60 s: his set's effect on his hits (`set-effect`) |\n| `al-kharid` | Al Kharid (the Desert's first stage since 0.8) as the Warrior for 60 s: the map built, a kill, the monsters animated |\n| `kalphite-lair` | Al Kharid as the Mage with Prayer, Hitpoints and Magic at 99 (not saved), the boss portal at 12 s: the Kalphite Queen's lair (`boss-arena`), her scripted Spines and Lightning, the first `kill` at 30 s calls her death off and she rises airborne out of the husk (`boss-second-form`), the second at 55 s kills her |\n| `wilderness` | The Wilderness (Megabonk's Graveyard) as the Warrior for 90 s: the starting crypt left (`crypt-left`), the map built, a kill, a PKer's ambush at 40 s and its spec (`pker-ambush`, `pker-spec`) |\n| `kbd-lair` | The Wilderness as the Mage with Prayer, Hitpoints and Magic at 99 (not saved), the boss door opened at 12 s: Megabonk's final stage, its final boss skinned as the King Black Dragon in his lair (`boss-arena`), his weighted attacks, killed at 75 s: \"Defeat the Final Boss\" and Megabonk's end-of-run portal |\n| `kbd-late` | The same with the boss door opened at 85 s, after a PKer's ambush, as Melty's runs do: the final stage and the KBD in his lair |\n| `ardougne` | Ardougne & Seers' Village (the Forest's second stage since 0.10, reached by the jump) as the Warrior for 60 s: the map built, a kill, the monsters animated |\n| `knights` | The same as the Mage with Prayer, Hitpoints and Magic at 99 (not saved), the boss portal at 12 s at Camelot, the arena's times x0.25: the Knights of the Round Table two and three at a time on the map (`arena-waves`), then Sir Lancelot (`arena-boss-released`), killed at 60 s |\n| `mole-name` | Lumbridge as the first map of a launch, the boss portal at 12 s: the boss bar names the Giant Mole, not another map's boss on the same Megabonk enemy (0.10.0's bug) |\n| `god-wars` | The God Wars Dungeon (the Forest's third stage since 0.11, built from OSRS level 2) as the Warrior for 60 s: the map built, a kill, the monsters animated |\n| `gwd-graardor`, `gwd-kree`, `gwd-kril`, `gwd-zilyana` | The same as the Mage with Prayer, Hitpoints and Magic at 99 (not saved), that general picked from the pool (`pool_boss`), the boss called at 8 s: his own room (`boss-arena`; Zilyana's on level 0), his three bodyguards at once (`arena-waves`), his scripted attacks (K'ril's prayer-piercing smash in the log), killed at 50 s |\n| `inventory` | Lumbridge as the Warrior: the whip, magic shortbow and Fire Strike given at 2-4 s and 12,345 gold at 5 s, the HUD at all four resolutions from 10 s, the prayer tab at 14 s and the inventory again at 20 s, 200,000 gold at 22 s: the OSRS side panel with the coins and the four weapons in the order taken (`osrs-inventory`), Megabonk's weapon slots gone, the prayer book, a white K stack |\n| `gwd-kril-melee` | K'ril as the Warrior for 90 s, the boss called at 6 s: his melee reaches the adventurer, and his Prayer smash goes through prayer and drains it (`pierces prayer` in the log), killed at 85 s |\n\n**Deaths in test runs** (`Systems/TestGuard.cs`): no blanket invulnerability, which would hide broken hits. Every hit\nlands in full through Megabonk's damage path; only one that would kill (`PlayerHealth.WillDamageKill`) is cut to\nleave 1 hitpoint, then the adventurer is healed to half and the save is logged (`lethal hit saved: <monster> hit for\nN with M left`; a hit that comes without its monster names Megabonk's damage source, `something (Enemy)`). `PlayerHealth.PlayerDied` and `KillPlayer` prefixes stop any other way of dying, logged by name. The\nguard is on in Melty's autoplay runs and scenarios (not in a hand-over test, where the creator plays), so Melty's\nrecordings no longer show a death and restart: the `death` scenario covers that. `\"deaths\": \"allow\"` turns it off. The report's `test guard:` line counts\nthe hits taken, their damage and the saves, and `player-hit` (optional) passes on the first monster hit that costs\nhealth, so a run where monsters never hurt the adventurer shows.\n\n**The dead-hook report** (`Core/DeadHooks.cs`): in test runs every method Bonkscape patches gets a counting prefix;\nthe report lists the patches that never ran (in the scenario report, and every 60 s in Melty runs' logs). A short run\nleaves situational ones (no chest opened, no death, no evade); a patch that never runs across scenarios built to\nreach it has been inlined by IL2CPP, as `WeaponBase.Use` and the casket's open were. A patch that was never applied\nisn't in the report at all: compare N in the log's `dead-hook report: counting calls to N patched methods` with the\ndistinct targets in [Architecture's patch table](architecture.md#harmony-patches) ([Gotchas](gotchas.md#il2cpp-and-harmony)).\n\n**The UI test** (not run every time; 0.12 builds on it): scenario `ui` sets each resolution in game\n(`Screen.SetResolution`; on a 2560x1600 desktop 3840x2160 clamps to 3840x1934) and, on the menu, opens each screen in\nits `\"menus\"` list through Megabonk's own `MainMenu.GoTo*` calls (`characters`, `maps`, `settings`, `unlocks`, `quests`,\n`shop`, `logs`, `leaderboards`, `credits`), screenshotting each. The report lists each screen's buttons the first time\n(`B_Back`, `B_Confirm`...); on character select it checks Bonkscape's levels panel against everything of Megabonk's a\nplayer sees there (texts by their first words, icons, buttons), not only the buttons: the first version, buttons only,\npassed a panel lying over the adventurer's description and weapon name. It also says whose model the preview shows\n(0.6: the adventurer's OSRS model, `CharacterSelect.Preview`; the panel sits in the free gap it measures between\nMegabonk's two windows, and passes at all four sizes). `\"adventurers\": true` in `\"ui\"` presses each phase-1 adventurer's\nbutton there too and screenshots it (`menu-characters-<id>-WxH`); a locked one (the Ironman, before total level 250)\nis reported as locked, as Megabonk keeps the last selection. In the run it screenshots the HUD at each\nresolution and checks Bonkscape's HUD boxes against the real minimap (`HudLayout.Boxes` and `Problems`: the orb cluster\ndrawn as one, the minimap as a circle). `bonk uilayout [w h ...]` runs the HUD check offline for eight resolutions. A\ntext's box can be wider than its letters, so a covered text is worth a look at the screenshot before it's called a\nbug.\n\n**Timings** (this PC, 2026-10-21; the old way is Melty's two-run test, as every version used):\n\n| | Melty test (old) | Local loop (0.3) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Change to result | about 7-8 min from upload (plus about a minute packaging, and reviewing 8 contact sheets) | 34-63 s per scenario, built and installed included |\n| Launch to the run | about 15 s, after Melty's install | 10.5-12 s (14.8 s before scenarios started 0.3 s after the menu) |\n| Reaching a boss fight | 75 s into a run (the boss call), one map a run | 12 s into the run (a spawn: 3 s), any map |\n| Map, stage, adventurer | the rotation picks | chosen per scenario |\n| Six scenarios | | 249 s in one launch (four: 182 s in four launches, 144 s in one) |\n| Screenshots | a frame every 2 s, after the run | full frames at chosen seconds, 0.25 s each |\n| Deaths | restart the run | saved and logged; real deaths only where a scenario asks |\n| Hooks that never ran | not reported | listed every run |\n\n## Testing by hand\n\n- `Dev.AutoStartRun = true` plays a Lumbridge run by itself on your own launches. Remember Melty switches BepInEx off\n  between its launches.\n- The log (`BepInEx/LogOutput.log`) has the useful lines: `OSRS assets ready in ... ms`, `Lumbridge built from the\n  OSRS cache in ...`, `Lumbridge arena: ...`, `sizing: ...`, `skin <enemy> (<tier>) as <name>: ...`, `check passed:`,\n  and every chat line as `[chat]`.\n- Offline: `bonk preflight` checks the sheets against the interop assemblies and both caches; `bonk worldscan`\n  checks every map region decodes; `bonk render` and `bonk world` make images to look at.\n\nWhat testing still lacks is on the [docket](docket.md).\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/TestMode.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Scenario.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/TestGuard.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Shot.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/DeadHooks.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Dev/Timing.cs tools/loop.py src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Controls.cs -->\n"}, {"section": "dev", "slug": "updating-docs", "text": "# How to update these docs\n\nThe `wiki-update` skill (`.claude/skills/wiki-update/`) runs this whole process, safely while other sessions commit\nto the same folder: start there after each version is built and its Melty test has a result.\n\n**The changelog and the wiki get updated with every version.** A version isn't finished until its changelog entry,\npatch notes and the wiki pages it touches say what the code now does. The wikis describe the code as it is, not the\nplan: a planned feature is marked planned until it's built.\n\n## With every commit\n\nA Claude Code hook (`.claude/hooks/bonkscape_after_commit.py`, registered in the user's own Claude Code settings for\nBash and PowerShell `git` commands, so it runs in every session whatever its folder) follows each commit to this repo\nand tells the session that made it:\n\n- **Docket:** unless the commit already changed [docket.md](docket.md), update the row of any task it starts,\n  finishes or blocks, add `todo` rows for follow-ups it turned up, set \"Last updated <date>, at <version>\", and commit\n  `docs/dev/docket.md` alone, by name. Game code is `done` only once it is in a version whose Melty test passed;\n  until then `doing`, with the commit hash. Tools and docs work is `done` when committed. Nothing changed on the\n  docket: leave it. When your task says another session owns the docket, list the row changes in your report\n  instead.\n- **Milestone plan:** when a row in a \"Milestone X.Y\" section changes, the release's Status in the \"at a glance\" table\n  of `docs/design/milestones.md` follows it: `todo`, `doing` once a row has started, `done` when every row is. The\n  docs check fails when the two disagree, and when a release is in one but not the other.\n- **Docs check:** the result of `check_docs.py --head --release`, the same check `package.py` runs; any FAIL is fixed\n  in a follow-up commit by the session that caused it.\n- **New version:** when the commit bumps `Plugin.Version`, to run the `wiki-update` skill once that version is built\n  and tested, or to ask the session that keeps BSWiki to.\n\nIt knows a commit by Claude Code's own record of it or by git's `[branch hash]` line. With neither (`git commit -q`,\nor a commit inside a chain that failed later) it takes Bonkscape's new HEAD when that commit's subject is in the\ncommand or first in the `-F` message file the command names, its Bash or PowerShell variables filled in (or, the\nweakest sign, the command names a file the commit\nchanged). It reports each commit once, says nothing for other commands, other sessions' commits or other\nrepositories, and never blocks a commit. A commit inside a failed chain is reported only once the user's settings\nregister the hook for `PostToolUseFailure` as well as `PostToolUse`; they don't yet. A crash in the hook shows as a\nfailed hook with its one-line error (exit 1), not as silence.\n\n## With every version\n\n1. **Changelog** ([changelog.md](changelog.md)): add the version at the top with\n   - the build date and time (local, UTC-5), the commit it was built from and the commit that holds its code,\n   - the Melty test id and result (update it when the test finishes or its footage is reviewed; the same result goes\n     in [the test history](testing.md#test-history)),\n   - **patch notes**: 1 to 4 short, player-facing bullets (paste these into the Melty release changelog),\n   - **changes**: what changed for developers, naming files, settings and sheet columns.\n2. **Wiki pages**: run the checker (below) and re-read every page it lists as touched by the changes. Fix what's no\n   longer true. New settings go on [Settings](../wiki/settings.md), new patches in\n   [Architecture](architecture.md#harmony-patches) with their `sheets/hooks.json` row, new gotchas in\n   [Gotchas](gotchas.md) and `docs/MODLOG.md`, new settings in `README.md`'s Settings table too (`package.py` checks).\n3. **Version lines**: the version and code commit at the top of [the wiki home](../wiki/index.md),\n   [the dev home](index.md) and [Architecture](architecture.md), and `documented_commit` in\n   `tools/wiki-site/docs_state.json`.\n4. **Statuses**: rows that were seen working in a Melty test become `verified` in their sheet; rows newly built become\n   `built`. Then regenerate the tables.\n5. **Docket** ([docket.md](docket.md)): move finished tasks to done, add what the test or review turned up.\n6. **Tables**: `python -I tools/wiki-site/gen_tables.py`.\n7. **Check**: `python -I tools/wiki-site/check_docs.py` must pass. `tools/package.py` won't build a release until\n   `--head --release` passes ([below](#the-docs-checker)).\n8. **Publish**: once the version's wiki-update is committed, the main Bonkscape session pushes master and BSWiki, the\n   wiki from a clean worktree of HEAD ([Hosting BSWiki](hub.md#hosting-bswiki)). The session that keeps the docs\n   commits and reports its hash; it pushes neither.\n\n## Generated tables\n\nTables of sheet data (weapons, monsters, gear, prayers, skills, waves, drops, UI and audio rows, hooks, the sheet list)\nare generated from `sheets/*.json`. In a page they sit between markers:\n\n```markdown\n<!-- gen:weapons -->\n(generated: don't edit by hand)\n<!-- /gen:weapons -->\n```\n\n`python -I tools/wiki-site/gen_tables.py` rewrites every marked table; `--check` only reports stale ones. To add a table,\nwrite a `t_<name>()` function in `gen_tables.py`, add it to `TABLES`, and put the markers in a page. Names come from\n`sheets/cache_assets.json`, so run the preflight first when OSRS ids changed.\n\n## The docs checker\n\n`python -I tools/wiki-site/check_docs.py` checks what can be checked mechanically, against the working tree. With\n`--at REV` it reads the code (`src/Bonkscape.Plugin`) from commit REV instead, which ignores other sessions'\nunfinished edits in the same folder and any code committed after REV; docs pages and sheets still come from the\nworking tree (when a check fails, it names the docs and sheets files with uncommitted changes). Use `--at <code commit>` when you document a released version (the commit that set its\n`Plugin.Version`), `--head` (the same as `--at HEAD`) for the latest commit, and the plain run to check uncommitted\nwork.\n\n**It gates every release:** `python -I tools/package.py <version>` runs `check_docs.py --head --release` first and\nrefuses to build the zip (non-zero exit, nothing written to `dist/`) when any check fails. `--release` leaves out the\nVersion check, because a version's changelog entry and wiki-home line are written after its build and Melty test (the\n`wiki-update` skill); every other check applies, so the session that adds a setting, patch, sheet column or self-check\ndocuments it in the same commit. Fix the FAIL lines and package again. `package.py --skip-docs` builds anyway, for\nemergencies only; it prints a warning, and the docs still need fixing.\n\n| Check | Fails when |\n|---|---|\n| Tables | A generated table is out of date. |\n| Settings | A `Config.Bind` key in `Plugin.cs` isn't on the Settings page, or its default there isn't the code's. |\n| Patches | A `[HarmonyPatch]` target has no row in Architecture's patch table, or a row in that table (its first column, `(args)` ignored) has no patch in the code. |\n| Sheet columns | A sheet column isn't mentioned in the Sheets reference. |\n| Self-checks | A name in `TestMode.Checks` isn't on the Testing page. |\n| Version | `Plugin.Version` has no changelog heading, or the wiki home doesn't name it. Skipped with `--release`. |\n| Docket | A task row's status isn't one of `todo`/`doing`/`done`/`blocked` (as `<span class=\"st st-X\">X</span>`), a task appears twice, a backticked `src/`, `sheets/`, `tools/` or `docs/` path doesn't exist, or the \"Last updated ..., at X.Y.Z\" line is missing or names a version newer than `Plugin.Version`. Without `--release` it must name `Plugin.Version` itself. |\n| Milestones | The milestone plan's \"at a glance\" table (`docs/design/milestones.md`) and the docket's \"Milestone X.Y\" sections name different releases, or disagree on whether one is `todo`, `doing` or `done`. |\n| Provenance | A source file under `src/` or `tools/` whose header says it was ported, adapted or is publish-gated isn't named in `docs/design/code-provenance.md`. |\n| Links | A relative link points to a missing page or heading (in `docs/wiki`, `docs/dev`, the milestone plan and the provenance page). |\n| Paths | A page BSWiki publishes (`docs/wiki`, `docs/dev`) holds a path from a PC the project is built on: a drive letter (a player's `Program Files` aside) or someone's home folder (a named folder under Windows' Users or Linux's home); URLs don't count, nor does a drive letter or home folder right after a word character. Name a sibling repo relative to this one (`../osrs-kit`) and a player's folders by their variables (`%LOCALAPPDATA%`, `$MEGABONK`). |\n\nIt also lists **pages to re-read**: every page ends with a `<!-- sources: ... -->` comment naming the code and sheets\nit describes. The checker compares those against the files changed since `documented_commit` (in\n`tools/wiki-site/docs_state.json`, which itself doesn't count) plus uncommitted changes; with `--at REV` or `--head`,\nonly the files changed from `documented_commit` to REV. A listed page isn't necessarily wrong; it's where to look.\nAfter re-reading them, set `documented_commit` to the commit you checked against.\n\nWhat it can't check: whether the prose is true. That takes reading the code (or a test run) for each listed page.\nThings only a run can show (whether Megabonk counts minibosses as bosses, how big things look) stay marked\nunconfirmed until a Melty run, its footage or a log line shows them.\n\n## Writing style\n\n- Say what the code does, with the numbers it uses. Quote in-game text exactly, in italics.\n- Mark anything planned with its phase (the generated tables do this from `status`).\n- Player pages (`docs/wiki`) explain how to play; developer detail (file names, hooks) goes in `docs/dev`.\n- Link pages with relative links (`[Prayer](prayer.md#prayer-points)`); the site and GitHub both follow them.\n\n## The site\n\n`python -I tools/wiki-site/server.py 8791`, then open <http://localhost:8791>. It renders `docs/wiki` and `docs/dev`\nstraight from disk, so a refresh shows your edits. The `bonkscape-wiki` entry in `.claude/launch.json` starts it from\nClaude Code's preview. Simplest: double-click `Bonkscape Hub.cmd` (or the Desktop shortcut), which starts the site and\nlinks its pages ([The hub](hub.md)).\n\n<!-- sources: tools/wiki-site/ .claude/hooks/bonkscape_after_commit.py -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "adventurers", "text": "# Adventurers\n\nYou play an OSRS adventurer in place of a Megabonk character. Each adventurer takes over one Megabonk character's\nslot: its place on character select, its movement and its hitbox. Bonkscape changes its name, stats, starting weapon,\nportrait and model.\n\n<!-- gen:characters -->\n| Adventurer | Style (attack style) | Starting weapon | Megabonk slot | Stat changes | Worn OSRS items | Unlock | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Warrior** | melee (aggressive) | Bronze scimitar | SirOofie | max HP +50, armour +0.15, move speed -0.02 | Rune full helm, Rune platebody, Rune platelegs, Dragon scimitar | always | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Ranger** | ranged (accurate) | Shortbow | Robinette | armour +0.05, move speed +0.075 | Green d'hide body, Green d'hide chaps, Magic shortbow | always | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Mage** | magic (accurate) | Wind Strike | Fox | move speed +0.04, luck +0.15 | Blue wizard hat, Blue wizard robe, Staff of fire | always | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Pure** | melee (aggressive) | Bronze scimitar | Ninja | max HP -40, damage +0.35, move speed +0.1 | Amulet of glory, Obsidian cape, Mithril gloves, Climbing boots, Zamorak monk top, Zamorak monk bottom, Dragon scimitar | levels:attack>=60,defence<=1 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Zerker** | melee (aggressive) | Dragon scimitar | Calcium | max HP +30, armour +0.08, damage +0.15 | Helm of Neitiznot, Fighter torso, Rune platelegs, Dragon defender, Dragon scimitar, Climbing boots, Amulet of glory | always | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Void Knight** | melee (accurate) | Void knight mace | Athena | max HP +25, armour +0.1, damage +0.1 | Void melee helm, Elite void top, Elite void robe, Void knight gloves, Void knight mace | always | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Durial321** | melee (accurate) | Abyssal whip | Vlad | max HP +40, armour +0.1, damage +0.05, move speed +0.03 | Green partyhat, Ahrim's robetop, Ahrim's robeskirt, Fire cape, Barrows gloves, Abyssal whip, Toktz-ket-xil, Climbing boots | always | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Ironman** | melee (defensive) | Bronze scimitar | SirChadwell | max HP +30, armour +0.1, luck +0.25 | Ironman helm, Ironman platebody, Ironman platelegs | total level 250 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Dharok** | melee (aggressive) | Dharok's greataxe | Dicehead | max HP +40, armour +0.12, damage +0.2, move speed -0.03 | Dharok's helm, Dharok's platebody, Dharok's platelegs, Dharok's greataxe | barrows set dharok | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Ahrim** | magic (accurate) | Ahrim's staff | Amog | max HP +20, armour +0.05, damage +0.1, luck +0.05 | Ahrim's hood, Ahrim's robetop, Ahrim's robeskirt, Ahrim's staff | barrows set ahrim | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Guthan** | melee (accurate) | Guthan's warspear | Spaceman | max HP +50, armour +0.12, damage +0.05 | Guthan's helm, Guthan's platebody, Guthan's chainskirt, Guthan's warspear | barrows set guthan | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Karil** | ranged (accurate) | Karil's crossbow | Bush | max HP +20, armour +0.05, damage +0.1, move speed +0.06 | Karil's coif, Karil's leathertop, Karil's leatherskirt, Karil's crossbow | barrows set karil | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Torag** | melee (defensive) | Torag's hammers | Bandit | max HP +45, armour +0.25 | Torag's helm, Torag's platebody, Torag's platelegs, Torag's hammers | barrows set torag | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Verac** | melee (accurate) | Verac's flail | Roberto | max HP +40, armour +0.12, damage +0.08 | Verac's helm, Verac's brassard, Verac's plateskirt, Verac's flail | barrows set verac | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n<!-- /gen:characters -->\n\nStat changes are Megabonk stat modifiers. For each stat in the row (max HP, armour, damage, size, attack speed, move\nspeed, luck), Bonkscape removes the Megabonk character's own modifier for that stat and adds the row's value, if it\nisn't zero. Move speed is added to Megabonk's move speed multiplier; the others are flat. Everything else about the\nMegabonk character is left as Megabonk made it. A [boss pet](monsters-and-bosses.md#boss-pets) out adds +1% luck to\nevery adventurer.\n\n## Unlocking\n\nSix adventurers are open from the start. The others are earned:\n\n- the **Pure**, once any one adventurer you've played has Attack 60 or more with Defence still 1;\n- the **Ironman**, once any one adventurer you've played (one with a skills save of its own) reaches a **total level**\n  of 250, all its skill levels added up;\n- each **Barrows brother** (Dharok, Ahrim, Guthan, Karil, Torag and Verac), once you own his full set: his helm, body,\n  legs and weapon, from the [Barrows chest](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-barrows-brothers).\n\nThese conditions replace Megabonk's own unlocks for the characters Bonkscape takes over, whatever your Megabonk\nprogress. Character select shows what a locked adventurer still needs, and refuses Megabonk's silver purchase of one,\nas in *\"Dharok can't be bought: his Barrows set (2/4).\"*\n\n## Special rules\n\n- **Durial321** carries a second weapon from the start: **Ice Barrage**, beside his Abyssal whip, without its 94\n  Magic requirement. He gets it again when a run restarts after a death.\n- **The Barrows brothers** join at level 70 in Attack, Strength, Defence and Hitpoints (Ahrim in Magic too, Karil in\n  Ranged), with their own weapon, and carry their set's effect on their hits\n  ([set effects](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-barrows-brothers)). Nothing is saved until a brother earns XP.\n  *(unconfirmed: needs a Melty run or footage)*\n- **The Pure never trains Defence**: its Defence XP is dropped, and its combat styles never include Defensive or\n  Controlled.\n- **The Ironman can't trade.** Megabonk's Shady Guy, and TzHaar-Hur-Tel standing in for him in TzHaar City, won't deal\n  with him: the prompt reads *Ironmen can't trade*, and pressing it says *\"The shopkeeper won't trade with an\n  Ironman.\"*\n\n## Starting weapons\n\nThe **Warrior, Ranger, Mage, Ironman and Pure** climb OSRS's weapon ladders by their own\n[levels](skills-and-xp.md): each starts with the highest tier its levels allow\n([Weapons](weapons-and-evolutions.md#weapon-ladders)), and once it meets a stronger weapon of its style past the\nladder's top, that one instead (the one whose highest requirement is highest: a weapon outside the ladders counts as\nsoon as that requirement is above the tier's). Everyone else keeps their own weapon whatever their levels.\n\n| Adventurer | Starts with | Climbs to |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warrior, Ironman, Pure (melee) | Bronze scimitar | the scimitar ladder by Attack (iron 2, steel 5, black 10, mithril 20, adamant 30, rune 40, dragon 60), then 70 Attack: **Abyssal whip**; 75 Attack: **Armadyl godsword**; 80 Attack and 90 Strength: **Scythe of vitur**. Also the **Void knight mace** once all its requirements are met (42 in Attack, Strength, Defence, Hitpoints, Ranged and Magic, 22 Prayer) while the ladder gives no more than the rune scimitar |\n| Ranger (ranged) | Shortbow | the shortbow ladder by Ranged (oak 5, willow 20, maple 30, yew 40, magic 50), then 55 Ranged: **Red chinchompa**; 75 Ranged: **Toxic blowpipe** |\n| Mage (magic) | Wind Strike | the Strikes by Magic (Water 5, Earth 9, Fire 13), then 75 Magic: **Trident of the seas**; 94 Magic: **Ice Barrage** |\n| Zerker | Dragon scimitar | his own, always |\n| Void Knight | Void knight mace | his own, always |\n| Durial321 | Abyssal whip | his own, always |\n| The Barrows brothers | their own weapon | their own, always |\n\nThe character select panel shows which weapon the adventurer will start with. A ladder's tier goes on when you pick\nthe adventurer and when a run starts, so a tier reached during a run comes with the next one (the chat box says so).\nA starting weapon past the ladder (the whip at Attack 70) is set when Megabonk loads its data; from the code, one\nreached during a play session may only reach a run after you restart Megabonk. That hasn't been tested yet\n([docket](../dev/docket.md)).\n\n> **Attack styles.** Each adventurer starts on its row's attack style (the Warrior, Zerker, Pure and Dharok\n> aggressive, the Ironman and Torag defensive, the rest accurate), which decides which skills its hits train; switch\n> it with **F1** or the combat tab ([Skills](skills-and-xp.md#attack-styles)). The style is kept per adventurer.\n\n## Looks\n\nEach adventurer is built from OSRS player parts in your cache: the male identity kits listed in the sheet (head, jaw,\ntorso, arms, hands, legs, feet) plus the worn items above, which hide the parts they cover, in the kits' own colours.\nIt plays OSRS's player animations:\n\n- **standing** (808) when you're still (the Barrows brothers but Torag have their own standing and walking animations);\n- **walking** (819) or **running** (824) by how fast you really move: running from 1.5 times OSRS's walking speed at\n  the model's size, each cycle sped up or slowed to match;\n- each weapon's OSRS **attack** when it fires ([Weapons](weapons-and-evolutions.md)), on the upper body while the legs\n  keep walking or running, the way the OSRS client merges them, with **that weapon in hand**: the adventurer is built\n  once per weapon (the weapon slot swapped for its item, the shield taken off for a two-handed one), so the weapon you\n  see is the one attacking. A weapon whose item has no worn model leaves the current one in hand;\n- a weapon's **special attack** animation, played whole ([Special attacks](weapons-and-evolutions.md#special-attacks));\n- the **death** animation (836) when the run ends in death.\n\nThe model follows your character, feet on the ground, and turns to face the way you move. Megabonk's own character\nmodel is hidden. Character select portraits are rendered from the same OSRS model, and on character select the\nselected adventurer's OSRS model stands in place of Megabonk's character, at its height, facing you, playing its\nstanding animation.\n\nIf a worn item isn't in your cache, the adventurer is built without it. If the body can't be built at all, you see\nMegabonk's character.\n\n## Size\n\nMegabonk's character height is measured the first time it stands upright, and again when you switch to another\nadventurer. Your adventurer is drawn at\nthat height times **`Models.AdventurerScale`** (default **1.6**, from 0.5 to 3). The maps are scaled to match, so the\nworld keeps OSRS proportions around you.\n\nYour **hitbox** is still Megabonk's: a bigger adventurer doesn't get hit more.\n\nMonsters are drawn on your adventurer's scale: an OSRS human stands about as tall as your adventurer, and goblins,\nrats and chickens keep their OSRS ratios to them ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#size)).\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Content.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/PlayerSkin.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Animator.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/CharacterSelect.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/Sizing.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/Osrs.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Plugin.cs sheets/characters.json sheets/weapons.json src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Barrows.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/SetEffects.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Unlocks.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Skills.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Pets.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/Rows.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/AttackStyles.cs -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "gear-passives", "text": "# Gear passives\n\nMegabonk's *tomes* (the passive upgrades offered on level-up) become OSRS gear. Each piece of gear takes over one\nMegabonk tome: its place in the level-up offers, its level cap and the one stat it raises. Bonkscape sets the stat,\nthe amount per level and the cap from the row, gives it the OSRS name and an icon rendered from your cache, and writes\nits description, for example *\"Amulet of fury (neck slot): +8% damage per level, up to level 8.\"*\n\n<!-- gen:passives -->\n| Gear | Slot | Megabonk tome | Stat | Per level | Max level | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Amulet of fury** | neck | Damage | damage (multiplication) | +8% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Berserker ring** | ring | Precision | crit chance (flat) | +7% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Barrows gloves** | hands | Cooldown | attack speed (addition) | +7.5% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Rune platebody** | body | Armor | armour (flat) | +12% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Boots of lightness** | feet | Agility | move speed (addition) | +15% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Ring of wealth** | ring | Luck | luck (flat) | +8% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Amulet of glory** | neck | Golden | coins gained (addition) | +12% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Kraken tentacle** | weapon upgrade | Size | attack size (addition) | +10% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Tome of fire** | shield | Duration | duration (addition) | +15% | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Godsword shard** | weapon upgrade2 | Knockback | knockback (addition) | +20% | 3 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Fire cape** | cape | Health | max HP (flat) | +25 | 8 | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n<!-- /gen:passives -->\n\n## One item per gear slot\n\nLike your OSRS equipment, each slot holds one item. Once you've picked a piece of gear, the other pieces for the same\nslot stop appearing in level-up offers:\n\n- **Neck**: Amulet of fury *or* Amulet of glory.\n- **Ring**: Berserker ring *or* Ring of wealth.\n- Every other slot has a single item.\n\nA piece also stops being offered once it reaches its max level (8; the Godsword shard's 3).\n\n## What's left out\n\nOn Bonkscape's maps, level-ups offer only the gear above, and the Fire cape only once you've earned it\n([below](#the-fire-cape)). Megabonk tomes without a row don't appear there.\n\nThree pieces matter for [evolutions](weapons-and-evolutions.md#evolutions): the **Kraken tentacle** (Abyssal whip\ninto Abyssal tentacle), the **Tome of fire** (Fire Strike into Fire Surge) and the **Godsword shard** (Armadyl godsword\ninto Armadyl godsword (i)).\n\n## The Fire cape\n\nThe Fire cape (cape slot, on Megabonk's Health tome: +25 maximum HP a level, up to 8) is earned. Killing\n[TzTok-Jad](monsters-and-bosses.md#tztok-jad) unlocks it for the adventurer you're playing, for good: the chat box\nsays *\"You have been awarded a Fire cape!\"*, and from then on level-ups can offer it to that adventurer. Each\nadventurer earns it separately. Unlocks are kept in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bonkscape\\unlocks.json`, beside the skill saves.\n*(unconfirmed: needs a Melty run or footage)*\n\nIts evolution, the Infernal cape, is planned; it can't happen yet.\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Content.cs sheets/passives.json src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Unlocks.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Combat.cs -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "getting-started", "text": "# Getting started\n\n## What you need\n\n- **Megabonk** on Steam.\n- **Old School RuneScape**, logged in at least once on this PC with RuneLite or the official client, so its game\n  files (the *cache*) exist. Bonkscape reads models, map data, icons, fonts and sounds from that cache and never writes\n  to it.\n- **Melty**, which installs the mod and its loader (BepInEx 6 for IL2CPP games) by itself.\n\n> **Your cache fills up as you play OSRS.** The OSRS client downloads most game files the first time it needs them.\n> If you've only logged in once, some monsters or items may be missing from your cache. Bonkscape then uses an OSRS\n> stand-in that *is* in your cache (another chicken for a chicken, for example; see\n> [Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#stand-ins)). If a map's regions are missing, you get Megabonk's own map (the\n> Forest, the Desert or the Graveyard) with OSRS scenery instead ([Maps](maps.md#when-a-map-isnt-built)).\n\n## Installing\n\nBonkscape is installed through Melty. It isn't public yet, so for now that means a test build from the creator.\n\nMelty puts BepInEx in Megabonk's folder and the mod in `BepInEx/plugins/Bonkscape/`. That folder holds:\n\n| File | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `Bonkscape.dll` | The mod |\n| `OsrsKit.Cache.dll` | The OSRS cache reader (from osrs-kit, Bonkscape's shared RuneScape library) |\n| `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `LICENSES/RuneLite-cache-BSD-2-Clause.txt` | Readme and licences |\n\nNo OSRS or Megabonk files are in the download. A `Bonkscape.Cache.dll` left there by an older version is no longer\nused and can be deleted.\n\n## Where Bonkscape looks for your OSRS files\n\nIt checks these folders in order and uses the first that holds a cache:\n\n1. The `OSRS.CacheFolder` setting ([Settings](settings.md)). When that's empty, the `OSRS_CACHE` line in\n   `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bonkscape\\settings.properties`, if there is one.\n2. The OSRS folder Melty passes in (the `OSRS_DIR` line in that same file), then `jagexcache\\oldschool\\LIVE` inside it.\n3. RuneLite's cache: `%USERPROFILE%\\.runelite\\jagexcache\\oldschool\\LIVE`.\n4. The official client's cache: `%USERPROFILE%\\jagexcache\\oldschool\\LIVE`.\n5. `%ProgramData%\\Jagex\\oldschool`.\n\nEvery file is opened read-only and shared, so OSRS can stay open while you play.\n\nIf none has a cache, the screen says *\"Bonkscape can't find Old School RuneScape's game files. Run OSRS once, then\nstart Megabonk again.\"* and the mod stays out of the way.\n\n## First launch\n\n1. Start Megabonk the way Melty tells you to. The first start after installing takes longer, because BepInEx\n   prepares itself.\n2. While the main menu loads, Bonkscape decodes what it needs from your cache in the background: monsters, the\n   adventurers, items and icons, sprites, fonts, sounds, then the arenas of every map and the bosses' lairs. Menus work meanwhile; the log line\n   `OSRS assets ready in ... ms` says how long it took.\n3. Megabonk's main menu shows as OSRS's login screen, its buttons running Megabonk's menu\n   ([HUD](hud-and-chat.md#title-screen)); press **Play**.\n4. On character select, the selected adventurer's OSRS model stands where Megabonk's character would, and a\n   panel above it shows that adventurer's name, combat level, skill levels and starting weapon\n   ([HUD](hud-and-chat.md#character-select-panel)).\n5. Pick an adventurer: **Warrior** (it takes Megabonk's `SirOofie` slot), **Ranger** (`Robinette`), **Mage** (`Fox`),\n   **Zerker** (`Calcium`), **Void Knight** (`Athena`) or **Durial321** (`Vlad`); the **Ironman** (`SirChadwell`), the **Pure** (`Ninja`) and the six\n   Barrows brothers are earned ([Adventurers](adventurers.md#unlocking)). Then start a\n   run on Megabonk's Forest map, which Bonkscape renames **Lumbridge & Swamp** (its later stages are Ardougne & Seers' Village and the God Wars Dungeon), or its Desert map, which becomes\n   **Al Kharid**, then Morytania and TzHaar City on its later stages ([Maps](maps.md#al-kharid)), or its Graveyard map, which\n   becomes **the Wilderness** ([Maps](maps.md#the-wilderness)). Bonkscape weapons and gear count as unlocked\n   whatever your Megabonk progress is.\n6. Behind OSRS's *Loading - please wait.* screen you're moved into the castle courtyard (on the Desert, Al Kharid). A chat line greets\n   you: *\"Welcome to Lumbridge. Press 1, 2 or 3 to pray against melee, missiles or magic.\"* (on another map, its\n   own name).\n\nOnly the Forest, the Desert and the Graveyard become OSRS maps. Megabonk's other maps aren't fully plain Megabonk, though: Bonkscape edits\nMegabonk's data once when the game loads, so the weapons, tomes and characters it reskins keep their OSRS names,\nicons and stats on every map, and the Megabonk enemies it remaps keep the OSRS rows' HP, damage and speed. On those\nmaps, monsters keep Megabonk's models and level-ups offer Megabonk's full pool\n([Known limitations](known-limitations.md)).\n\n## Playing on a controller\n\nMegabonk's own controls work on a gamepad as before. Bonkscape's actions also answer to a controller button, beside\ntheir keys, on buttons Megabonk leaves free in a run:\n\n| Action | Key | Controller | Setting |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Protect from Melee, Missiles, Magic | 1, 2, 3 | d-pad left, up, right | `Controller.Prayers` |\n| Prayer book or inventory | F5 | d-pad down | `Controller.PrayerTab` |\n| Special attack | F | Y | `Controller.SpecFire` |\n| Arm the next weapon's special attack | Q | left stick click | `Controller.SpecSwitch` |\n| Camera near or far | V | right stick click | `Controller.CameraToggle` |\n| Next attack style | F1 | Back | `Controller.AttackStyle` |\n\nThey're buttons of Rewired's standard gamepad layout (Megabonk reads its input through Rewired), so they mean the same\non any controller Rewired sees as a gamepad. They do nothing while a Megabonk window has the game paused. Change them\nin [Settings](settings.md); `None` turns one off. Test runs press them through Bonkscape's test tools, not on a real\ncontroller: whether a real pad's presses reach Bonkscape is *(unconfirmed: needs a run with a controller)*.\n\n## Leaderboards\n\nRuns with Bonkscape loaded never upload scores to Megabonk's Steam leaderboards.\n\n## Where your progress lives\n\n- Skills: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bonkscape\\skills_<adventurer>.json`, one per adventurer ([Skills and XP](skills-and-xp.md#saving)).\n- Unlocks (the Fire cape, Barrows pieces, pets and the pet out) and each adventurer's attack style:\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bonkscape\\unlocks.json`.\n- Settings: `BepInEx/config/gg.melty.bonkscape.cfg` in Megabonk's folder ([Settings](settings.md)).\n- Log: `BepInEx/LogOutput.log`. Bonkscape's lines start with `[Info   : Bonkscape]`, and every chat message is\n  logged too, as `[chat] ...`.\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Plugin.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Content.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Hud.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Patches/CorePatches.cs tools/package.py src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/CharacterSelect.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Unlocks.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/TitleScreen.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Controls.cs -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "glossary", "text": "# Glossary\n\n## Old School RuneScape\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **OSRS** | Old School RuneScape, Jagex's MMO. Bonkscape reads its game files from your PC. |\n| **Cache** | The folder of game files the OSRS client keeps (`main_file_cache.dat2` and `.idx` files). It fills up as you play, because the client downloads things the first time it needs them. |\n| **Gielinor** | The OSRS world. |\n| **Tile** | One square of the OSRS map. Positions like (3222, 3218) are OSRS world tiles: x east, y north. |\n| **Region** | A 64 x 64-tile block of the map, the unit the cache stores map data in. |\n| **NPC** | A non-player character, including every monster. Each has a numbered definition in the cache. |\n| **Identity kit** | One of the body parts (head, torso, legs...) a player model is built from. |\n| **Combat style** | Melee, ranged or magic. |\n| **Special attack (spec)** | A weapon's special move, paid for with special attack energy. |\n| **Attack style** | Which skills your hits train: melee Accurate (Attack), Aggressive (Strength), Controlled (all three) or Defensive (Defence); ranged Accurate, Rapid or Longrange; magic Casting or Defensive casting. |\n| **Weapon ladder** | OSRS's tiers of one weapon (bronze to dragon scimitars), each needing a higher level. |\n| **Protection prayer** | A prayer that blocks one combat style: Protect from Melee, Missiles or Magic. |\n| **Hitsplat** | The small marker with a damage number over whoever was hit. Red for damage, blue for 0. |\n| **Side panel** | OSRS's panel of tabs in the lower right: in Bonkscape, the combat tab, the inventory and the prayer book. |\n| **XP drop** | The `+XP` that floats up the screen when you gain experience. |\n| **Game tick** | OSRS's 0.6-second heartbeat. Bonkscape groups XP drops per tick. |\n| **Combat level** | One number summing up your combat skills, worked out with OSRS's formula. |\n| **Total level** | All of a character's skill levels added up. |\n| **PKer** | A player killer: a player who attacks others in the Wilderness. In Bonkscape, an ambushing enemy in a player's gear. |\n| **Ironman** | An OSRS account that plays alone, without trading. Bonkscape's Ironman can't buy from shops. |\n| **Barrows brothers** | Six undead brothers buried at the Barrows in Morytania: Dharok, Ahrim, Guthan, Karil, Torag and Verac. Each wears an armour set with its own effect. |\n| **Set effect** | What a full Barrows set does on its wearer's hits, such as Guthan's healing. |\n| **Casket** | An OSRS reward container. In Bonkscape, Megabonk's chests. |\n| **Clue scroll** | An OSRS treasure trail: a scroll of steps (dig here, search there, talk to someone) that ends in a reward casket. |\n| **Pet** | A boss's rare drop that follows you around. |\n| **Tokkul** | The TzHaar's currency. In TzHaar City, Megabonk's gold. |\n| **Sequence** | An OSRS animation: frames that move a model's parts (stand, walk, run, attack, death). |\n| **Interleave** | How the OSRS client plays an attack on the upper body while the legs keep walking or running. |\n| **Spot animation (spotanim)** | A graphic effect such as a spell's projectile or impact. Bonkscape uses them for spell projectiles and the level-up fireworks. |\n\n## Megabonk\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Run** | One game from character select until you die or win. |\n| **Stage** | One part of a map's run; the Forest and the Desert have several each. |\n| **Tome** | Megabonk's passive upgrades. Bonkscape turns them into OSRS gear. |\n| **Level-up offer** | The choice of upgrades Megabonk shows when you level up. |\n| **Elite / miniboss / boss** | Bigger, stronger enemies. Megabonk enlarges them; Bonkscape caps how much of that it passes on. |\n| **Boss portal** | The object you use to call a stage's boss. |\n| **Final portal** | The boss portal of a map's last stage; it calls the final boss. |\n| **Shrine** | A Megabonk interactable that offers a reward. |\n| **Swarm** | A timeline event that spawns a crowd of one kind of enemy. |\n| **Hitbox** | The invisible shape that decides whether something hits. Bonkscape never changes hitboxes, only how things look. |\n\n## Bonkscape\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Adventurer** | Your OSRS character: Warrior, Ranger, Mage, Zerker, Void Knight, Durial321, Ironman, Pure or a Barrows brother. |\n| **Arena** | A real OSRS map built from your cache (Lumbridge, Ardougne & Seers' Village and the God Wars Dungeon on the Forest; Al Kharid, Morytania and TzHaar City on the Desert; the Wilderness on the Graveyard; and\nthe bosses' lairs) that replaces the stage's ground. |\n| **Fallback stage** | Megabonk's own map (the Forest, the Desert or the Graveyard) dressed in OSRS scenery, used when the arena can't be built. |\n| **Gear passive** | A piece of OSRS gear that replaces a Megabonk tome. One per gear slot. |\n| **Evolution** | A maxed weapon plus its gear passive becoming its OSRS upgrade when a casket opens. |\n| **Stand-in** | Another OSRS NPC used when a monster's own model isn't in your cache. |\n| **Style icon** | The Attack, Ranged or Magic icon over a monster, showing its combat style. |\n| **Sheet** | One of the design data files in `sheets/*.json`. Every weapon, monster, prayer and so on is a row. |\n| **Phase** | Which milestone a row belongs to. Phase 1 is in the mod now; phases 2 and 3 are later. |\n| **Status** | A row's state: *todo* (planned), *built* (in the mod) or *verified* (confirmed in a Melty test run). |\n| **Preflight** | The `bonk preflight` tool that checks every sheet against Megabonk and an OSRS cache before a build. |\n| **Melty** | The service that installs, tests and publishes the mod. |\n| **Test mode / autoplay** | How Melty's automated test plays a run by itself. |\n| **Self-check** | A named result the mod reports to Melty during a test run, such as `prayer-switched`. |\n\n<!-- sources: sheets/ -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "hud-and-chat", "text": "# HUD and chat\n\nBonkscape draws an OSRS HUD over Megabonk's, laid out like OSRS's own interfaces and drawn with OSRS's sprites and\nfonts, read from your cache. In a run on one of Bonkscape's maps it hides the Megabonk pieces it replaces: the health bar (on the HUD\nand under your adventurer), the XP bar (its *LVL n* text stays), the monsters' health bars and its weapon slots (the\ntome slots stay). They come back when the\nrun ends, so Megabonk's other maps keep their own HUD. Megabonk's timer, kill counter and minimap stay (the minimap\nshows the map's own OSRS minimap). Everything scales with the screen height in whole steps of 540 px: 1x at 720p, 2x at\n1080p, 3x at 1440p, 4x at 4K. Above 720p the chat box is one step smaller.\n\nThe `Debug.OsrsHud` setting turns it off, and with `Debug.HideMegabonkHud` off Megabonk's own bars stay up beside it\n([Settings](settings.md)).\n\n## In a run\n\n| Element | Where | What it shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Minimap orbs** | Left of Megabonk's minimap, as in OSRS | From the top: the XP button, the **hitpoints** orb (your Megabonk HP), the **prayer** orb (your prayer points; lit while a prayer is on) and the **special attack** orb: your special attack energy in %, lit when the armed weapon's spec can fire, with that weapon's icon beside it ([Weapons](weapons-and-evolutions.md#special-attacks)). Megabonk's dash keeps its key, but its cooldown no longer shows. Each orb drains from the top; its number is green when full, yellow at half and red when empty. |\n| **XP counter** | Left of the orbs | Your total XP over all skills, and under it a green strip: Megabonk's progress to its next run level. |\n| **XP drops** | Rising from under the XP counter into it | The skill icon and the amount, for about 2 s, grouped per 0.6 s tick ([Skills](skills-and-xp.md#xp-drops-and-level-ups)). Drops from the same tick line up under each other. |\n| **Health bars** | Over a monster's head, and over yours | OSRS's green-over-red bar. A monster's shows for 6 s after you hit it: 30 px wide, 50 for minibosses, 80 for bosses. Yours shows whenever you're below full health, and for 6 s after any hit. |\n| **Style icons** | Over the 10 nearest monsters, above their health bar | The Attack, Ranged or Magic skill icon for the monster's combat style ([Prayer](prayer.md)) |\n| **Hitsplats** | On the body, at half its height as in OSRS: on a monster you hit, and on you when you're hit | OSRS's red hitsplat with the damage (any damage shows as at least 1), the green poison hitsplat for Megabonk's poison damage, or the blue 0 for a hit that missed: a prayer block, or a Megabonk evade or Aegis block. Hits Megabonk's short hit cooldown stops show nothing, and so do hits on a monster already dead. Each lasts 1.2 s, in OSRS's four slots (middle, above, above left, above right); a fifth hit adds its damage into the newest. Megabonk's own floating damage numbers (yours and the monsters') are hidden on Bonkscape's maps. |\n| **Side panel** | Lower right | OSRS's side panel with two tabs: the inventory and the prayer book ([below](#the-side-panel)) |\n| **Overhead prayer** | Above your adventurer's health bar | The OSRS overhead icon of the active protection prayer |\n| **Prayer glint** | On the overhead prayer icon | A small sparkle for 0.45 s when your prayer stops or halves a hit |\n| **Minimap frame and compass** | Round Megabonk's minimap | OSRS's minimap frame, with the compass in its socket turning as the camera turns. Drawn only when Bonkscape could measure where Megabonk's minimap is on screen. |\n| **Skilling markers** | Over usable trees, rocks and fishing spots within 10 tiles | The skill's icon (Woodcutting, Mining or Fishing), with a progress bar under it while you gather; gone while the spot comes back ([Skilling](skilling.md)) |\n| **Coins** | The side panel's inventory, first slot | The OSRS coin stack for your amount, the amount in OSRS's colours ([The side panel](#the-side-panel)); in TzHaar City the Tokkul stack ([Maps](maps.md#tzhaar-city)). Megabonk's own gold number and icon are hidden. |\n| **Chat box** | Bottom left corner | See [below](#chat-box) |\n| **Level-up** | Chat box, and at your feet | OSRS's level-up dialogue covers the chat box for 3 s: *\"Congratulations, you just advanced a Strength level.\"*, *\"Your Strength level is now 41.\"* and *\"Click here to continue\"*, with the skill's icon. OSRS's level-up fireworks play at your adventurer's feet, and keep playing while Megabonk's level-up window pauses the game. |\n| **Evolution** | Chat box | The new weapon's name, like *\"Fire Surge!\"*, as a dialogue for 3.5 s |\n| **Special attack graphics** | At your feet or the target's | The spec's OSRS graphic, rendered from your cache |\n| **Loading screen** | The whole screen | Black with OSRS's *Loading - please wait.* box: when a run starts, after a death restart and when a boss fight moves into its lair, until you stand on the OSRS map ([Maps](maps.md#when-its-built)) |\n| **Death message** | Centre | *\"Oh dear, you are dead!\"* in large red OSRS text. Megabonk's death screen title says it too. |\n| **Clue hint** | Under the minimap, while a clue trail is going | The current step's hint; OSRS's yellow hint arrow over the spot and an arrow on the minimap ([Clue scrolls](monsters-and-bosses.md#clue-scrolls)) |\n| **Hint** | Above the side panel, first 45 s | *\"Pray against the icon over a monster: 1 Melee  2 Missiles  3 Magic  (F5: prayers, F1: attack style)\"* |\n\nBonkscape doesn't draw a run clock: Megabonk's own timer stays.\n\nWhile one of Megabonk's windows is up over the run (the level-up offers, the pause menu, a chest, the Shady Guy, a\nshrine, the death screen, or anything that pauses the game), the OSRS HUD steps aside but for the chat box, so\nnothing lies over the window or Megabonk's Stats and Inventory panels beside it.\n\n## The side panel\n\nOSRS's side panel sits in the lower right, with three working tabs at OSRS's places (the other tab stones are\nempty, as on Tutorial Island):\n\n- **Combat**: the weapon of your style in hand, your combat level, your [attack styles](skills-and-xp.md#attack-styles)\n  as OSRS's buttons with the current one lit, *\"XP: Attack, Strength, Defence\"* for it and *\"F1: next style\"*. Click a\n  style to pick it.\n\n- **Inventory**: your money in the first slot, as OSRS's coin stack for the amount, with the amount in OSRS's colours\n  (yellow, then white with K from 100,000, green with M from 10 million); in TzHaar City the Tokkul stack. Then each\n  weapon you hold, in the order you took it, as its OSRS item (an evolved weapon as its evolution's), with its\n  Megabonk level in the corner where OSRS draws a stack's number. Megabonk's own gold counter and weapon slots are\n  hidden.\n- **Prayer book**: the three protection prayers at their places in OSRS's prayer book, each with its key in the\n  corner; the active one on OSRS's glow and its key in yellow. Out of points, the prayers that aren't on are dimmed.\n  Your prayer points show under the book, as *\"43 / 52\"*.\n\n**F5** (`Interface.PrayerTabKey`, OSRS's own key for the prayer book) switches between the prayer book and the\ninventory; **F1** (`Interface.AttackStyleKey`) moves to the next attack style and shows the combat tab for 4 s; or click\na tab when Megabonk shows the cursor. On a controller, d-pad down switches the tab and Back moves the style. Each run\nstarts on the inventory.\n\n## Title screen\n\nWith `Interface.TitleScreen` on, Megabonk's main menu shows as OSRS's login screen, read from your cache: its\nbackground, and a combined logo with BONK over OSRS's *SCAPE* and *OLD SCHOOL*, with Megabonk's splash line under it.\nThe login box says *\"Welcome to Bonkscape\"* and holds Megabonk's main menu functions as its buttons: **Play**,\n**Shop**, **Unlocks**, **Quests**, **Settings** and **Exit**, with **Leaderboards**, **Credits**, the language and\n**Discord** under it. Each runs Megabonk's own; a yellow *!* marks a Megabonk alert. Megabonk's silver shows at the top\nright, and the lower right corner names both versions, as *\"Bonkscape 0.14.2 - Megabonk ...\"* (the running ones). **Enter** or **Space**\nplays. When one of Megabonk's other screens or windows opens, it shows as Megabonk's own.\n\nThe title's music is *Scape Main*, OSRS's login track, on builds with the music engine (not release builds), with\nOSRS's mute button in the box's lower right.\n\n## Megabonk's windows\n\nWith `Interface.OsrsWindows` on, Megabonk's windows (its menus, the pause menu, the level-up offers, the shop) take\nOSRS's look: its stone for their backgrounds and buttons (the buttons darker), its two-line brown border\nfor their frames, no drop shadows. Colours that mean something, such as Megabonk's blue for something you can buy and\nits rarity colours, stay. They work as before: only their look changes. A chest window keeps its own teal background\n([Known limitations](known-limitations.md)).\n\n## Chat box\n\nOSRS's chat box, cut down: it shows your last **4** lines, and only for 8 seconds after something new is said, then\nhides. It sits in the bottom left corner, only as tall as its lines. A level-up or evolution dialogue covers it while\nit shows. The same message twice in a row becomes one line with a count, like *\"You get some logs. (x3)\"*.\n\nWhat it says:\n\n| When | Message |\n|---|---|\n| A run starts | *Welcome to Lumbridge. Press 1, 2 or 3 to pray against melee, missiles or magic.* (on the Desert: *Welcome to Al Kharid. ...*; on the Graveyard: *Welcome to The Wilderness. ...*) |\n| A later stage is another map | *Welcome to Ardougne & Seers' Village.* / *Welcome to God Wars Dungeon.* / *Welcome to Morytania.* / *Welcome to TzHaar City.* |\n| A skill levels up | *Congratulations, you just advanced a Mining level. You are now level 12.* |\n| A level brings a weapon into reach | *You can now wield the rune scimitar (from your next run).* / *You can now cast Fire Strike.* |\n| You switch attack style | *Attack style: Longrange (Ranged, Defence XP).* |\n| You eat | *You eat the lobster. It heals some health.* |\n| You drink a prayer potion | *You drink some of your prayer potion.* |\n| Prayer runs out | *You have run out of Prayer points, you can recharge at an altar.* |\n| You pray with no points | *You need to recharge your Prayer at an altar.* |\n| A weapon evolves | *Your Fire Strike has become a Fire Surge!* |\n| You press `Q` (`Special.SwitchKey`) or its controller button | *Special attack: Abyssal whip (Energy Drain).* (the newly armed weapon) |\n| You press `F` without enough energy, or with nothing in reach | *You don't have enough special attack energy left.* / *There's nothing in reach.* |\n| An Ironman tries to trade | *The shopkeeper won't trade with an Ironman.* |\n| You press `V` (`Camera.ToggleKey`) or its controller button | *Camera: far.* / *Camera: near.* |\n| The Giant Mole digs | *The Giant Mole burrows into the ground!* |\n| A boss fight moves into its lair | *You follow the Giant Mole down into its lair.* (or *the Kalphite Queen*) |\n| The Kalphite Queen's first form falls | *Kalphite Queen rises again!* |\n| A PKer ambushes you in the Wilderness | *Ice Tank is attacking you!* (its name) |\n| You open the Wilderness's boss door | *You climb down towards King Black Dragon.* |\n| You enter the Fight Caves, or a God Wars general's room | *You enter TzTok-Jad's arena; the waves come first.* (or the general's name) |\n| You beat the hidden Barrows brother | *You find Dharok's helm in the Barrows chest!* (the piece you got) |\n| A brother's set is complete | *You have Dharok's full set: he can join you as an adventurer.* |\n| You press Megabonk's Buy on a locked adventurer | *Dharok can't be bought: his Barrows set (2/4).* |\n| A boss joins after its waves | *TzTok-Jad appears!* / *Sir Lancelot appears!* |\n| TzTok-Jad drops below half health | *TzTok-Jad calls for help!* |\n| A boss you kill unlocks gear | *You have been awarded a Fire cape!* |\n| Lava first burns you in a run | *The lava's heat burns you!* |\n| You pick up a clue scroll | *You find a clue scroll!*, then *You read the clue scroll: 3 steps on Lumbridge & Swamp.* and the first hint |\n| You finish a clue step | *You dig and find another clue (2 of 3).* (or *search*, *speak*), then the next hint |\n| You finish a clue trail | *You dig and find a reward casket: 150 coins!* |\n| A boss drops its pet | *You have a funny feeling like you're being followed.* (one you own already: *... like you would have been followed...*) |\n| You gather | *You get some logs.* / *You manage to mine some tin.* (the rock's ore; *stone* for plain rocks) / *You catch some shrimps.* |\n\nEvery chat line is also written to the BepInEx log as `[chat] ...`.\n\n## Character select panel\n\nOn character select, a panel at the top of the screen, in the gap between Megabonk's two windows and\nabove your adventurer, on OSRS's stone background, shows (drawn a step smaller when it wouldn't fit):\n\n- the **name** of the adventurer whose button you're on, as OSRS's skills tab shows the player's, and their\n  **combat level**, locked ones included, as *\"Combat Lvl: 3\"*\n  ([Skills](skills-and-xp.md#combat-level)),\n- that adventurer's skills in two columns, each with its icon and level, the panel as wide as its longest skill name,\n- *\"Starts with: Dragon scimitar\"*, the weapon that adventurer will start with\n  ([Adventurers](adventurers.md#starting-weapons)), and for a Barrows brother how much of his set you own, as\n  *\"(set 4/4)\"*; for a locked adventurer, what unlocks them instead: *\"Locked: total level 250 on one adventurer\n  (180)\"* (the best you have) or *\"Locked: his Barrows set (2/4)\"*.\n\nOnce you own a [boss pet](monsters-and-bosses.md#boss-pets), a pet line under the panel shows the pet out, *\"Pet:\n...\"* with its icon; clicking it (*\"Click to change\"*) brings out the next one you own, then none.\n\n## When your cache can't be found\n\nIf Bonkscape finds no OSRS cache, or decoding it fails, the screen shows *\"Bonkscape can't find Old School\nRuneScape's game files. Run OSRS once, then start Megabonk again.\"*\n\n## Sounds\n\nOSRS sound effects are synthesised from your cache's instrument data and mixed into Megabonk's audio, at\n`Audio.OsrsVolume` ([Settings](settings.md)). They play for: getting hit (and the different sound for a 0), hitting\nmonsters (at most every 0.12 s) and killing them (every 0.2 s), each weapon's attack (at most every 0.3 s per\nweapon), level-ups and evolutions, each prayer going on, prayer off, running out of prayer, picking up coins, eating\nand drinking, chopping, mining and fishing.\n\nOSRS **music**: an experimental player plays the map's track (Lumbridge: *Harmony*; Al Kharid: *Al Kharid*; Morytania: *Village*; TzHaar City: *Mor Ul Rek*; the Wilderness: *Wilderness*; Ardougne & Seers' Village: *Knightly*; the God Wars Dungeon: *Armageddon*) from your cache in place of Megabonk's music\n(`Audio.OsrsMusic`), but only with its engine, `OsrsKit.Music.dll`, which release builds don't include; without it\nMegabonk's music plays. The [title screen](#title-screen) plays *Scape Main* the same way. OSRS's jingles (level-up,\ndeath) aren't built.\n\n<!-- gen:audio -->\n| Row | Event | OSRS sound or track | Fallback | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `enemy_hit` | hit | `sfx:2498` | megabonk | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `player_hit` | hit | `sfx:510` | megabonk | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `player_zero` | hit | `sfx:511` | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `kill` | kill | `sfx:2498` | megabonk | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `pickup_item` | pickup | `sfx:2582` | megabonk | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `pickup_coins` | pickup | `sfx:3924` | megabonk | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `level_up` | level_up | `sfx:3925` | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `level_up_jingle` | level_up | `jingle:level_up` | sfx:3925 | <span class=\"st st-todo\">planned (phase 2)</span> |\n| `prayer_off` | prayer_off | `sfx:2663` | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `prayer_empty` | prayer_off | `sfx:2672` | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `chop` | pickup | `sfx:2735` | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `mine` | pickup | `sfx:3220` | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `death` | death | `jingle:dead` | megabonk | <span class=\"st st-todo\">planned (phase 2)</span> |\n| `lumbridge_music` | map_music | `track:Harmony` | megabonk | <span class=\"st st-todo\">planned (phase 2)</span> |\n<!-- /gen:audio -->\n\n## UI rows\n\nThe OSRS sprites and fonts each element uses (`sheets/ui.json`):\n\n<!-- gen:ui -->\n| Row | Element | OSRS sprite / font | Text | Position | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `hitsplat_damage` | hitsplat | `1359` | {damage} | over_target | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `hitsplat_zero` | hitsplat | `1358` | 0 | over_target | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `hitsplat_poison` | hitsplat | `1360` | {damage} | over_target | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `hitsplat_font` | hitsplat | `font:p11_full` | digits | over_target | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `xp_drop` | xp_drop | `font:p11_full` | +{xp} | top_right | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `chat_line` | chat_line | `font:p12_full` | Congratulations, you just advanced a {skill} level. | bottom_left | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `chat_box` | chat_line | `1017` | none | bottom_left | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `death_screen` | death_screen | `font:b12_full` | Oh dear, you are dead! | center | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `coin_stack` | coin_stack | `item:995` | {gp} | hud_gold | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `overhead_icon` | overhead_icon | `440` | none | over_player | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `style_marker_melee` | style_marker | `197` | none | over_enemy | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `style_marker_ranged` | style_marker | `200` | none | over_enemy | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `style_marker_magic` | style_marker | `202` | none | over_enemy | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `run_timer` | run_timer | `font:p12_full` | {mm}:{ss} | hud_timer | <span class=\"st st-todo\">planned (phase 3)</span> |\n| `prayer_orb` | overhead_icon | `font:p11_full` | Prayer {points} | hud_prayer | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `missing_cache` | chat_line | `font:p12_full` | Bonkscape can't find Old School RuneScape's game files. Run OSRS once, then start Megabonk again. | center | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| `orb_frame` | hud_sprite | `1071` | minimap orb frame | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `orb_empty` | hud_sprite | `1059` | drained part of an orb | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `orb_hitpoints` | hud_sprite | `1060` | hitpoints orb fill | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `orb_poison` | hud_sprite | `1061` | poisoned hitpoints orb fill | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `orb_prayer` | hud_sprite | `1063` | prayer orb fill | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `orb_prayer_on` | hud_sprite | `1066` | prayer orb fill while praying | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `orb_special` | hud_sprite | `1607` | special attack orb fill | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `orb_special_on` | hud_sprite | `1608` | special attack orb fill, on | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `icon_hitpoints` | hud_sprite | `1067` | hitpoints orb icon | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `icon_prayer` | hud_sprite | `1068` | prayer orb icon | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `icon_prayer_on` | hud_sprite | `1058` | prayer orb icon while praying | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `icon_special` | hud_sprite | `1610` | special attack orb icon | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `xp_button` | hud_sprite | `1196` | XP button beside the orbs | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `counter_back` | hud_sprite | `297` | stone tile of the XP counter and panels | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `total_xp` | hud_sprite | `222` | XP counter icon | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `prayer_glow` | hud_sprite | `4892` | active prayer glow | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `minimap_frame` | hud_sprite | `1177` | resizable-mode minimap frame | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `compass` | hud_sprite | `169` | compass face | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `health_bar_30` | hud_sprite | `2176` | 30 px health bar (green) | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `health_bar_30_back` | hud_sprite | `2177` | 30 px health bar (red) | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `health_bar_50` | hud_sprite | `2178` | 50 px health bar, minibosses (green) | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `health_bar_50_back` | hud_sprite | `2179` | 50 px health bar (red) | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `health_bar_80` | hud_sprite | `2182` | 80 px health bar, bosses (green) | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `health_bar_80_back` | hud_sprite | `2183` | 80 px health bar (red) | hud | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| `dialogue_font` | font | `font:q8_full` | level-up dialogue (interface 233) | chatbox | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n<!-- /gen:ui -->\n\n`hitsplat_poison` is drawn for a weapon hit Megabonk marks as poison; no test run has shown one yet *(unconfirmed:\nneeds a Melty run or footage)*. `run_timer` is planned\n(phase 3); Megabonk's own timer shows meanwhile.\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Projectiles.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Skilling.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Music.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Hud.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/HudLayout.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/OsrsUi.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/MegabonkHud.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/LevelUpFx.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Chat.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Evolutions.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/CharacterSelect.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Combat.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Loot.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Sfx.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/OsrsAudio.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Patches/CorePatches.cs sheets/ui.json sheets/audio.json src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/BossFight.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/LavaBurn.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Unlocks.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Arena.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/Runner.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/Osrs.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Barrows.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/SecondForm.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Pkers.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Graveyard.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Inventory.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/TitleScreen.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/OsrsWindows.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/TitleArt.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Pets.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Clues.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/AttackStyles.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Controls.cs -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "index", "text": "# BSWiki\n\nBonkscape is a Megabonk mod that puts an Old School RuneScape adventurer into a Megabonk horde-survival run through\nreal OSRS places: Lumbridge, Ardougne & Seers' Village, the God Wars Dungeon, Al Kharid, Morytania, TzHaar City and the\nWilderness. The maps, monsters, items, icons, fonts and sound effects are read from\nyour own OSRS install while the game starts. Nothing from either game ships with the mod.\n\nThis wiki says how each system works **in the code as it is now**. It doesn't describe plans as if they were built.\n\n> **Version:** BSWiki matches Bonkscape **0.16.1** (code at `f716b88`). What changed in each version:\n> [patch notes](../dev/changelog.md). How each version tested: [test history](../dev/testing.md#test-history).\n\n## Pages\n\n| Page | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| [Getting started](getting-started.md) | What you need, installing through Melty, the first launch |\n| [Maps](maps.md) | Lumbridge, Ardougne & Seers' Village, the God Wars Dungeon, Al Kharid, Morytania, TzHaar City and the Wilderness: what's built from your cache, spawn, portals, lava, minimap, see-through walls |\n| [Adventurers](adventurers.md) | The fourteen adventurers: unlocks, special rules, starting weapons, stats, looks and size |\n| [Weapons and evolutions](weapons-and-evolutions.md) | Every weapon, its Megabonk behaviour and level-ups; caskets and the three evolutions |\n| [Gear passives](gear-passives.md) | The OSRS gear that replaces Megabonk's tomes, one item per slot, and the Fire cape |\n| [Monsters and bosses](monsters-and-bosses.md) | Who spawns when, combat styles, sizes, the Giant Mole's dig, the Knights of the Round Table, the God Wars generals, the Kalphite Queen, the Barrows brothers, the Fight Caves and TzTok-Jad, PKers and the King Black Dragon, boss pets, clue scrolls |\n| [Prayer](prayer.md) | Protection prayers: keys, drain, how much they block, potions, the prayer bar |\n| [Skills and XP](skills-and-xp.md) | OSRS's XP table, what trains what, saving, unlocks by level, combat level |\n| [HUD and chat](hud-and-chat.md) | Hitsplats, XP drops, chat box, the inventory and prayer book, the title screen, Megabonk's windows, the character select panel |\n| [Skilling](skilling.md) | Trees, rocks and fishing spots, and what they give |\n| [Settings](settings.md) | Every config key, its default and what it does |\n| [Known limitations](known-limitations.md) | What isn't there yet, or behaves oddly |\n| [Glossary](glossary.md) | OSRS, Megabonk and Bonkscape terms |\n\n## Status labels\n\nEvery system comes from a row in the design sheets (`sheets/*.json`), and every row has a status. The wiki shows it\nthe same way everywhere:\n\n- <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span>: built and confirmed working in a Melty test run (self-checks or footage).\n- <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span>: in the mod, not yet confirmed in a test run.\n- <span class=\"st st-todo\">planned (phase 2)</span>: designed in the sheets but **not built**. Phase 2 and 3 are later\n  milestones; phase 1 is what's in the mod now.\n\n## In one minute\n\n- You start a run on Megabonk's first map. With Bonkscape loaded it's **Lumbridge & Swamp**: you appear in the\n  castle courtyard, and the castle, the River Lum, the church, the fields and the swamp are built from your cache.\n  Its second stage is **[Ardougne & Seers' Village](maps.md#ardougne-and-seers-village)**, up to Camelot, and its third\n  **[the God Wars Dungeon](maps.md#the-god-wars-dungeon)**, where one of the four generals waits.\n  Megabonk's Desert becomes **[Al Kharid](maps.md#al-kharid)**, then **[Morytania](maps.md#morytania)** (Canifis,\n  Mort Myre and the Barrows) and **[TzHaar City](maps.md#tzhaar-city)**, whose final portal leads into the Fight\n  Caves. The Graveyard becomes **[the Wilderness](maps.md#the-wilderness)**, with the King Black Dragon below it.\n- You play one of fourteen [adventurers](adventurers.md): the Warrior, Ranger, Mage, Zerker, Void Knight or Durial321,\n  the Ironman or the Pure once you've earned them, or a Barrows brother once you own his set. Level-ups offer\n  [OSRS weapons](weapons-and-evolutions.md) and [OSRS gear](gear-passives.md) in place of Megabonk's.\n- [Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md) in Lumbridge are chickens, goblins, giant rats, guards, cows and giant frogs,\n  with the Giant Mole as the boss. Ardougne & Seers' Village ends with the Knights of the Round Table, the God Wars\n  Dungeon with one of its four generals, and Al Kharid with the two-form Kalphite Queen. Morytania has its snails,\n  ghouls, banshees, vampyres and the Vyrewatch, with a hidden Barrows brother as the boss and the others as minibosses;\n  TzHaar City has the TzHaar, and the Fight Caves' waves end with TzTok-Jad, whose attacks the right prayer blocks\n  completely; the Wilderness has revenants, PKers and the King Black Dragon. Each monster fights in melee, ranged or\n  magic, shown by the icon over its head.\n- Press **1 / 2 / 3** to [pray](prayer.md) against melee, missiles or magic, and **F** for your weapon's\n  [special attack](weapons-and-evolutions.md#special-attacks) (**Q** picks which weapon's). On a controller they're on\n  the d-pad, Y and a left-stick click ([Playing on a controller](getting-started.md#playing-on-a-controller)).\n- Damage trains your OSRS [skills](skills-and-xp.md), and they're kept between runs. Your levels climb OSRS's weapon ladders (bronze to dragon scimitars,\n  normal to magic shortbows, Wind to Fire Strike) and open stronger weapons, and **F1** picks your attack style.\n- Stand next to the maps' trees, rocks and fishing spots to [skill](skilling.md) for coins, buffs and food.\n\nDeveloper documentation (architecture, sheets, gotchas, testing, changelog, task docket) is in [BSWiki's developer pages](../dev/index.md).\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Plugin.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Controls.cs -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "known-limitations", "text": "# Known limitations\n\nWhat isn't there yet, or behaves differently from what you'd expect. The [docket](../dev/docket.md) tracks the work.\n\n## Not built yet\n\n- **Music.** Release builds play Megabonk's music: the experimental OSRS music player needs its engine\n  (`OsrsKit.Music.dll`), which isn't shipped yet. OSRS's jingles (level-up, death) aren't built.\n- **Prayer altars.** The chat tells you to recharge at an altar, but there's none; prayer potions are the only refill.\n- **More adventurers, weapons, gear, monsters and maps.** The Dwarf multicannon (phase 3) and the Vyrewatch Sentinel\n  (phase 2) are designed but not in the game.\n- **The Infernal cape.** The Fire cape's evolution is in the evolutions sheet, but it can't happen yet.\n- **The Fight Caves monsters' own abilities.** Tz-Kek don't split, Yt-MejKot don't heal, and Tok-Xil and Ket-Zek\n  attack as the Megabonk enemies under them.\n- **The Mole's own drop** (the Mole claw). Planned.\n\n## Works, with caveats\n\n- **Controllers.** Bonkscape's controller buttons are read through Rewired's standard gamepad layout, so a controller\n  Rewired doesn't see as a gamepad gets none (its keys still work). Test runs press them through the test tools,\n  never on a real pad: whether a real controller's presses reach Bonkscape is *(unconfirmed: needs a run with a\n  controller)*. The buttons are changed in the settings file only ([Playing on a\n  controller](getting-started.md#playing-on-a-controller)).\n- **Your cache decides what you see.** The OSRS client downloads models on demand. Monsters missing from your cache use\n  stand-ins; if a map's regions are missing, you get Megabonk's own map (the Forest, the Desert or the Graveyard) instead.\n- **Megabonk's other maps change a little too.** Bonkscape edits Megabonk's data when the game loads, so its reskinned\n  weapons, tomes and characters keep their OSRS names, icons and stats on every map, and the Megabonk enemies it remaps\n  keep the OSRS rows' HP, damage and speed.\n- **Minibosses and bosses use Megabonk's stats** (times their map's difficulty) and, all but TzTok-Jad, the Kalphite\n  Queen, the King Black Dragon, Sir Lancelot and the God Wars generals, Megabonk's own\n  attacks; only ordinary monsters take the sheet's HP, damage and speed.\n- **Attack styles change only the XP.** In OSRS they also change accuracy, damage or speed; here they decide only\n  which skills your hits train.\n- **The Barrows brothers fight as Megabonk's boss and minibosses**, with their attacks; only the set effects are\n  OSRS's. Torag's set slows instead of draining run energy, which Megabonk doesn't have.\n- **Deep Freeze's ice block draws solid.** The ice it leaves on its target shows as an opaque tan box, not OSRS's\n  see-through ice (seen in Melty test footage).\n- **Weapon tiers change between runs.** A ladder tier reached during a run comes with the next run; a starting weapon\n  past the ladder (the whip at Attack 70) is set when Megabonk loads, so it may only take effect after a restart (not\n  yet tested).\n- **Projectiles go through the maps' walls**, yours and the monsters', because walls are only solid to bodies.\n- **Textured surfaces use one colour**, the average of their OSRS texture.\n- **Spell casts don't stop you.** In OSRS you stand still to cast; here the cast plays on the upper body while your\n  legs keep moving.\n- **Megabonk's chest window** keeps its own teal background; the other windows take OSRS's stone and borders.\n- **Hitboxes are Megabonk's.** Megabonk's enemy hitboxes are tall capsules, often taller than the OSRS model drawn\n  for them, so a hit can land a little outside a monster's shape.\n- **The King Black Dragon's breaths** freeze for 3 s (6 s in OSRS), and his shocking breath slows you instead of\n  draining your combat stats. Protect from Magic stands in for an anti-dragon shield against his fire.\n- **Megabonk's minibosses and portals** come from the Forest's, the Desert's and the Graveyard's own timelines and placement rules\n  (the Desert's last stage has Megabonk's final portal); Bonkscape only picks which OSRS monsters they are and moves\n  the portal.\n\n## Melty test runs\n\nThese only show up in Melty's automated test runs (autoplay):\n\n- Autoplay presses no controller buttons: it drives Bonkscape's actions directly.\n- Autoplay checks two tiles ahead on the map's grid and turns along walls; whether that stops it pressing into\n  walls is *(unconfirmed: needs footage)*.\n- Autoplay plays the adventurers this PC has unlocked in turn, and the maps in turn (Lumbridge, the Wilderness,\n  Al Kharid, Morytania, Ardougne & Seers' Village, the God Wars Dungeon, TzHaar City). A run on a later stage (Ardougne & Seers' Village, the God Wars Dungeon, Morytania, TzHaar City) starts on its Megabonk map's first stage\n  and moves to its own a second in;\n  since its adventurer arrives without the levels the earlier stages bring, monsters there get their HP and hits\n  without Megabonk's stage scaling.\n- Autoplay always takes the first level-up offer, so most weapons aren't exercised. It opens chests, and a test\n  shortcut readies the first evolution.\n- 1:15 into a test run (test runs last 2 minutes), the adventurer is moved to the boss portal and opens it, so each\n  test meets a stage boss (on the Wilderness it opens the boss door, never while a window has the game paused). The\n  first PKer comes 40 s in. On TzHaar City it opens the final portal 40 s in, and the Fight Caves run at a quarter of\n  their times (a wave every 5 s, Jad at 30 s), so the waves and Jad fit in; the God Wars Dungeon's final portal opens\n  40 s in too, for its general's waves. Autoplay switches prayer when Jad starts\n  an attack, as a player reading his wind-up would.\n- A test run's 6th kill always drops a clue scroll, so each test shows a clue trail.\n- A hit that would kill the adventurer in a test run leaves 1 hitpoint instead (then heals to half), so a test run\n  doesn't die; every other hit lands in full.\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/ sheets/ -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "maps", "text": "# Maps\n\nBonkscape has seven maps, each built from **your own OSRS cache** in place of a Megabonk map or stage:\n\n- **Lumbridge & Swamp** on Megabonk's Forest <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> (Melty publish test of\n  0.1.11: courtyard start, castle, river and bridges, swamp, minibosses, death and restart).\n- **[Ardougne & Seers' Village](#ardougne-and-seers-village)** on the Forest's second stage: from East Ardougne's\n  market to Seers' Village and Camelot.\n- **[The God Wars Dungeon](#the-god-wars-dungeon)** on the Forest's third stage: the icy cavern under Trollheim,\n  where the four gods' armies fight.\n- **[Al Kharid](#al-kharid)** on Megabonk's Desert: the desert town, with [the Kalphite Lair](#the-kalphite-lair) as its\n  boss arena.\n- **[Morytania](#morytania)** on the Desert's second stage: Canifis, Mort Myre and the Barrows.\n- **[The Wilderness](#the-wilderness)** on Megabonk's Graveyard: from the Edgeville ditch north, with\n  [the King Black Dragon's lair](#the-king-black-dragons-lair) below it.\n- **[TzHaar City](#tzhaar-city)** on the Desert's third stage: the TzHaar's underground city of obsidian and lava,\n  with [the Fight Caves](#the-fight-caves) as its boss arena.\n\nWhich one you play is the Megabonk map you pick: the Forest is Lumbridge, then Ardougne & Seers' Village and the God Wars Dungeon; the Desert's three stages are Al Kharid,\nMorytania and TzHaar City; the Graveyard is the Wilderness. The rest of this page describes Lumbridge first; the other maps work the same\nway except where their sections say otherwise.\n\n## The Lumbridge arena\n\nThe arena is the real Lumbridge, built from **your own OSRS cache**: OSRS world tiles **(3140, 3140) to (3266, 3300)**,\n127 x 161 tiles across 16 map regions. That takes in the castle and its courtyard, the River Lum and its bridges, the\nchurch, the houses, the fields and Lumbridge Swamp, with about 11,800 placed OSRS objects.\n\nWhat's built:\n\n- **Terrain**: tile heights, colours and lighting like the OSRS client draws them, shaped overlay tiles (paths,\n  floors, shorelines), and water. Textured surfaces use the average colour of their OSRS texture.\n- **Scenery**: walls, buildings, trees, fences, rocks and the rest, turned and mirrored the way the client places them.\n- **Collision**: see [What blocks you](#what-blocks-you).\n- **Minimap**: Lumbridge's own OSRS minimap, rendered from the cache, replaces Megabonk's minimap, zoomed in 2.5x\n  towards OSRS's scale (`Minimap.Zoom`). Its map icons (trees, rocks, and map functions such as banks or altars) stay\n  upright as the minimap turns, as in OSRS. Every other map and boss lair shows its own minimap the same way.\n- **See-through walls**: see [below](#see-through-walls).\n- **Draw distance**: scenery and walls more than 200 m from your adventurer aren't drawn (`Map.DrawDistance`; 0\n  draws everything); the ground always is. They're still solid.\n\nMegabonk still runs the run itself. It generates the Forest stage first (its timeline, chests, shrines and boss\nportal), then Bonkscape switches the Forest's ground, props and grass off and puts Lumbridge in their place.\n\n### How it's sized\n\nThe arena is scaled to your adventurer: one OSRS tile has the same size relative to your adventurer as in OSRS. The\nadventurer is drawn larger than Megabonk's character (`Models.AdventurerScale`, 1.6 by default), and Lumbridge grows\nwith it, so there's room to run. See [Adventurers](adventurers.md#size).\n\n### When it's built\n\n1. While the main menu loads, every map's world data (tiles, scenery, colliders) and minimap are built in the\n   background from your cache, Lumbridge first, then the other maps and the bosses' lairs (the Giant\n   Mole's, the Kalphite Lair, the Fight Caves, the King Black Dragon's and the four God Wars generals' rooms); their meshes are then made\n   a few at a time, at most 8 ms of each frame.\n2. When a run starts, the screen goes black with OSRS's *Loading - please wait.* box, so Megabonk's own stage never\n   shows. Bonkscape waits 0.3 s, then for Megabonk's spawn intro to finish (your character on the ground and done\n   teleporting, at most 8 s more), and places Lumbridge under you.\n3. You're moved into the courtyard, and any monster already alive is moved next to you. The loading box stays up one\n   more second while Megabonk's camera swings in, and the camera turns to face north, looking down 45 degrees\n   (`Camera.NearPitch`). A restart after a death, and a move into or out of a boss's lair, do the same.\n\n### Spawn, chests, shrines and the boss portal\n\n| What | Where |\n|---|---|\n| **You** | The castle courtyard, OSRS tile (3222, 3218), facing west towards the castle keep. |\n| **Chests (caskets) and shrines** | Random open ground: a walkable tile that has no roof, at least 3 tiles in from the edge, at least 6 tiles from the spawn and 3 tiles from each other. They're placed again every run. |\n| **Boss portal** | The dark hole in Lumbridge Swamp, OSRS tile (3169, 3172), the way down to the Lumbridge Swamp Caves in OSRS. |\n| **Monster spawns** | Anywhere on the arena (Megabonk's spawn area is set to the arena, minus a 2-tile margin). |\n| **Skilling spots** | On Lumbridge's own trees, rocks and shores ([Skilling](skilling.md)). |\n\nIf you ever fall through the world (25 m below Lumbridge's lowest ground), you're put back in the courtyard.\n\n### What blocks you\n\nMegabonk needs room to run, so less blocks you than in OSRS:\n\n- **Blocks**: building walls, scenery that's 2 x 2 tiles or bigger, tree trunks (thin, so you can run between trees),\n  and water, except on bridges.\n- **Doesn't block**: fences, railings, hedges, stiles, gates, doors, curtains, ladders, stairs, bushes, plants,\n  flowers, crates, barrels, sacks, tables, chairs, stools, benches, signs, and any other 1 x 1 clutter.\n\n- **Also blocks**: square scenery such as fountains and statues blocks as a round pillar, so its corners don't catch\n  you; tiles with no ground at all (holes into the void) are walled off on every map.\n\nYou and the monsters bump into these, but Megabonk's camera, its spawn checks and **projectiles pass through them**.\nYour adventurer climbs slopes up to 62 degrees, steeper than Megabonk's own limit, for OSRS's steps and hills. Hold a\ndirection without moving for a quarter of a second and you slide along the wall in front of you, or hop up the ledge\nwhen nothing is in front at knee height.\nMonsters find their way around walls on the map's tile grid ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#how-monsters-move)).\n\n### See-through walls\n\nOSRS's camera looks into rooms from above; Megabonk's sits behind your shoulder. So Lumbridge's walls are split into\ncells of 8 x 8 tiles, and every frame any wall cell between Megabonk's player camera and your adventurer is hidden.\nIt stays solid while hidden.\n\n### Stages\n\nMegabonk's Forest map has three stages: **Lumbridge & Swamp**, then\n[Ardougne & Seers' Village](#ardougne-and-seers-village), then [the God Wars Dungeon](#the-god-wars-dungeon).\n\n## Ardougne and Seers' Village\n\n**Ardougne & Seers' Village** takes over the **second stage** of Megabonk's Forest. Its arena is OSRS world tiles\n**(2496, 3264) to (2783, 3535)**, 288 x 272 tiles: from East Ardougne's market north past the farms and guilds to\nSeers' Village and Camelot, so the detail budget applies ([Morytania](#morytania)).\n\n| What | Where |\n|---|---|\n| **You** | East Ardougne, OSRS tile (2662, 3305). The chat box says *\"Welcome to Ardougne & Seers' Village.\"* |\n| **Boss portal** | Camelot, OSRS tile (2758, 3507). The fight stays on the map: the Knights of the Round Table come in waves, then Sir Lancelot ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-knights-of-the-round-table)). |\n| **Monsters** | Its own roster, with Heroes and Paladins as minibosses ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#ardougne-and-seers-village)). |\n| **Chests, shrines, monster spawns, skilling spots** | As on Lumbridge, on its own ground, trees, rocks and shores. |\n\nIts music (experimental) is *Knightly*.\n\n## The God Wars Dungeon\n\nThe **God Wars Dungeon** takes over the **third stage** of Megabonk's Forest, its last. Its arena is OSRS world tiles\n**(2816, 5248) to (2943, 5375)**, 128 x 128 tiles, built from OSRS's level 2 there (the cavern floor), as an\nunderground map: lit as a cave and walled off wherever it's blocked.\n\n| What | Where |\n|---|---|\n| **You** | OSRS tile (2881, 5310). The chat box says *\"Welcome to God Wars Dungeon.\"* |\n| **Final portal** | OSRS tile (2846, 5334). It loads Megabonk's final stage, whose final boss is this run's general, in his own room ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-god-wars-generals)). |\n| **Monsters** | The four gods' armies, with spiritual warriors and mages as minibosses ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-god-wars-dungeon)). |\n| **Chests, shrines, monster spawns, skilling spots** | As on Lumbridge; chests and shrines may stand on roofed ground too, as in a lair. |\n\nIts music (experimental) is *Armageddon*.\n\n### The generals' rooms\n\nEach run one of the four generals is picked at random, and the final stage puts you in his room, built from your\ncache: General Graardor's, Kree'arra's and K'ril Tsutsaroth's on level 2, Commander Zilyana's on level 0. His death\nends the run, as any final boss's does. *(unconfirmed: needs a Melty run or footage)*\n\n## Al Kharid\n\nAl Kharid takes over the **first stage** of Megabonk's Desert. Its arena is OSRS world tiles **(3264, 3110) to\n(3391, 3330)**, 128 x 221 tiles: the desert town and the sand around it.\n\n| What | Where |\n|---|---|\n| **You** | OSRS tile (3329, 3220). The chat box says *\"Welcome to Al Kharid. Press 1, 2 or 3 to pray against melee, missiles or magic.\"* |\n| **Boss portal** | OSRS tile (3323, 3319). It leads into [the Kalphite Lair](#the-kalphite-lair). |\n| **Monsters** | Al Kharid's desert roster, with Kalphite Guardians as minibosses ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#al-kharid)). |\n| **Chests, shrines, monster spawns, skilling spots** | As on Lumbridge, on Al Kharid's ground and its own trees, rocks and shores. |\n\nIts music (experimental) is *Al Kharid*.\n\n### The Kalphite Lair\n\nOpening Al Kharid's boss portal moves the fight into the Kalphite Lair, the Kalphite Queen's cave, as the Giant Mole's\nportal moves it into its lair: OSRS tiles (3456, 9472) to (3519, 9535), 64 x 64 tiles, from your cache, under the\nloading screen. You land at OSRS tile (3486, 9515), the Queen stands at (3476, 9498), and the chat box says *\"You\nfollow the Kalphite Queen down into its lair.\"* ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-kalphite-queen)).\n\n## Morytania\n\nMorytania takes over the **second stage** of Megabonk's Desert. Its arena is OSRS world tiles **(3400, 3158) to (3783, 3607)**,\n384 x 450 tiles, about eight times Lumbridge's area: Canifis, Mort Myre swamp and the Barrows.\n\n| What | Where |\n|---|---|\n| **You** | Canifis, OSRS tile (3494, 3488). |\n| **Boss portal** | The Barrows, OSRS tile (3565, 3290). Its boss is the run's hidden Barrows brother ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-barrows-brothers)). |\n| **Monsters** | Morytania's own roster and waves ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#when-monsters-come)). |\n| **Chests, shrines, monster spawns, skilling spots** | As on Lumbridge, on Morytania's ground and its own trees, rocks and shores. |\n\nWhat's different from Lumbridge:\n\n- **A dark sky.** The Desert stage's fog and sky turn a dark purple (`maps.sky`), and its sunlight and ambient light\n  lose the desert's warm tint.\n- **A second stage.** Megabonk's own scaling for a later stage makes its monsters tougher than on Al Kharid.\n- **Built at load.** If a stage starts before its map's build is done, it waits behind the loading screen: up to 45 s\n  for the build, and for its meshes until it has waited 30 s in all; past 45 s the stage stays Megabonk's own.\n- **A detail budget.** Maps bigger than 40,000 tiles leave out ground decorations (flowers, grass tufts, small\n  stones) and 1 x 1 scenery that blocks nothing and has fewer than 300 faces (`Map.LargeMapMinFaces`; 0 keeps\n  everything). Trees and ore rocks are always kept, for skilling. Lumbridge is under the limit, so it keeps everything.\n- **Raised houses.** Canifis's houses stand on stilts, as in OSRS: their floors, walls, windows and furniture are at\n  the raised level, with the posts underneath.\n- **Stages.** The Desert's first stage is [Al Kharid](#al-kharid) and its third [TzHaar City](#tzhaar-city).\n- **The welcome line**, reaching it from Al Kharid, reads *\"Welcome to Morytania.\"*\n\n## TzHaar City\n\nTzHaar City takes over the **third stage** of Megabonk's Desert, the map's last: you reach it after Al Kharid and\nMorytania, and the stage is named *TzHaar City*. Its arena is OSRS world tiles **(2432, 5056) to (2559, 5183)**, 128 x\n128 tiles.\n\n| What | Where |\n|---|---|\n| **You** | OSRS tile (2480, 5167). The chat box says *\"Welcome to TzHaar City.\"* |\n| **Final portal** | The Fight Cave entrance, OSRS tile (2439, 5170). The Desert's last stage has Megabonk's final portal instead of a boss portal; it leads into [the Fight Caves](#the-fight-caves). |\n| **Monsters** | The TzHaar, with TzHaar-Ket champions as minibosses ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#tzhaar-city)). |\n| **Chests, shrines, monster spawns, skilling spots** | As on Lumbridge, on TzHaar City's ground and its own scenery. Chests and shrines may stand on roofed ground too, as in a lair. |\n\nWhat's different:\n\n- **Underground.** It's built and lit as a cave, like a boss's lair: darker, black beyond its walls, no sky, and\n  walled off wherever it's blocked.\n- **Lava burns.** Lava tiles block you, as in OSRS. Standing pressed against a lava tile's edge (within 0.35 of a\n  tile) burns 2.4% of your maximum health every OSRS tick (0.6 s), 4% a second. It goes through Megabonk's own damage,\n  so it shows as a hitsplat, and no prayer stops it. The first burn of a run says *\"The lava's heat burns you!\"* The\n  middle of a one-tile bridge is half a tile from the lava on each side, so walking across is safe. *(unconfirmed:\n  needs a Melty run or footage)*\n- **Tokkul instead of coins.** Megabonk's gold is drawn as OSRS's Tokkul, on the ground, flying to you and in your\n  [inventory](hud-and-chat.md#the-side-panel)'s first slot.\n- **TzHaar-Hur-Tel.** Megabonk's Shady Guy, the merchant, is drawn as TzHaar-Hur-Tel (NPC 2183), as tall as your\n  adventurer. Like the Shady Guy, he won't trade with an [Ironman](adventurers.md#special-rules). *(unconfirmed: needs a Melty run or footage)*\n- **Harder monsters.** Its difficulty is 1, but as on any later stage, Megabonk's own stage scaling multiplies every\n  monster's HP and damage on top.\n\n### The Fight Caves\n\nOpening the final portal moves the fight into the Fight Caves, TzTok-Jad's arena, the way the Giant Mole's portal\nmoves it into its lair: OSRS tiles (2368, 5056) to (2431, 5119), 64 x 64 tiles, from your cache, under the loading\nscreen. You land at OSRS tile (2413, 5115) and the chat box says *\"You enter TzTok-Jad's arena; the waves come first.\"*\nSix timed waves of Fight Caves monsters come first; TzTok-Jad joins after them, at OSRS tile (2399, 5087)\n([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#tztok-jad)). Its lava burns as the city's does.\n\n## The Wilderness\n\nThe Wilderness takes over Megabonk's **Graveyard**. Its arena is OSRS world tiles **(2944, 3520) to (3391, 3967)**,\n448 x 448 tiles, from the Edgeville ditch north, so the detail budget applies ([Morytania](#morytania)).\n\n| What | Where |\n|---|---|\n| **You** | OSRS tile (3090, 3525). The chat box says *\"Welcome to The Wilderness. Press 1, 2 or 3 to pray against melee, missiles or magic.\"* |\n| **Boss door** | OSRS tile (3014, 3849): Megabonk's surface crypt door, prompting *Climb down to King Black Dragon*. It leads into [the King Black Dragon's lair](#the-king-black-dragons-lair). |\n| **Monsters** | Revenants and green dragons, the Chaos Elemental and the Revenant maledictus as minibosses, and PKers who ambush you ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-wilderness)). |\n| **Chests, shrines, monster spawns, skilling spots** | As on Lumbridge, on the Wilderness's ground and its own trees, rocks and shores. |\n\nWhat's different:\n\n- **No crypt.** A Graveyard run begins in Megabonk's maze crypt; Bonkscape takes you out by its exit door at once,\n  behind the loading screen, so the stage clock starts, and the crypt's objectives (*Find the exit*) don't show.\n- **No coffins, keys or lamps.** Megabonk's own way to the Graveyard's boss gives way to the boss door.\n- **Music** (experimental): *Wilderness*.\n\n### The King Black Dragon's lair\n\nOpening the boss door says *\"You climb down towards King Black Dragon.\"* and loads Megabonk's final stage of the\nGraveyard, as TzHaar City's final portal does on the Desert. That stage's final boss is the King Black Dragon, and the\nfight moves into his lair, OSRS tiles (2240, 4672) to (2303, 4735), from your cache: you land at OSRS tile (2258, 4687)\nand he stands at (2271, 4699) ([Monsters](monsters-and-bosses.md#the-king-black-dragon)). His death ends the run, as\nany final boss's does. *(unconfirmed: needs a Melty run or footage)*\n\n## When a map isn't built\n\nYou get Megabonk's own map instead (the Forest, the Desert or the Graveyard), dressed in OSRS scenery, when:\n\n- the `Map.RealLumbridge` setting is off: it covers every map and lair ([Settings](settings.md)),\n- your cache is missing any of the map's regions (the log says *\"Lumbridge isn't in this OSRS cache\"*, or\n  *\"Morytania ...\"*),\n- the map's build isn't done 45 s into its run, or\n- building it failed for any other reason (logged as *\"Lumbridge arena failed, keeping Megabonk's stage\"*, or the\n  map's own name).\n\nIn the fallback, Megabonk's trees, rocks and bushes are drawn with the OSRS models in the map's own row: for\nLumbridge, Tree (with an Oak tree about 30% of the time), Tin rocks and Bush, at sensible sizes, keeping Megabonk's\nown colliders. Morytania's and the God Wars Dungeon's rows list none, so their fallback is Megabonk's plain stage.\nSkilling spots are placed around where you start: 10 trees, 8 rocks and 4 fishing spots, 12 to 70 m away.\n\n## Planned maps\n\nEvery map in `sheets/maps.json`, built or planned:\n\n<!-- gen:maps -->\n| Map | Megabonk map | OSRS area (x0, y0, x1, y1) | Music (experimental) | Minutes | Special rule | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Lumbridge & Swamp** | Forest stage 0 | 3140, 3140, 3266, 3300 | Harmony | 10 | none | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Morytania** | Desert stage 1 | 3400, 3158, 3783, 3607 | Village | 9 | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **The Wilderness** | Graveyard stage 0 | 2944, 3520, 3391, 3967 | Wilderness | 16 | pkers_revenants | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **God Wars Dungeon** | Forest stage 2 | 2816, 5248, 2943, 5375 | Armageddon | 8 | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **TzHaar City** | Desert stage 2 | 2432, 5056, 2559, 5183 | Mor Ul Rek | 8 | none | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Al Kharid** | Desert stage 0 | 3264, 3110, 3391, 3330 | Al Kharid | 10 | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Ardougne & Seers' Village** | Forest stage 1 | 2496, 3264, 2783, 3535 | Knightly | 9 | none | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n<!-- /gen:maps -->\n\nEach map has an OSRS track (Lumbridge: *Harmony*; Al Kharid: *Al Kharid*; Morytania: *Village*; TzHaar City: *Mor Ul\nRek*; the Wilderness: *Wilderness*; Ardougne & Seers' Village: *Knightly*; the God Wars Dungeon: *Armageddon*). An experimental player plays it from your\ncache (`Audio.OsrsMusic`), but only with its engine, which release builds don't include, so they play Megabonk's\nmusic ([HUD and chat](hud-and-chat.md#sounds)). More boss arenas are on the [docket](../dev/docket.md).\n\n<!-- sources: src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Arena.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/MapProps.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Skilling.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Content.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/Osrs.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Core/Runner.cs sheets/maps.json src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/LavaBurn.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/BossFight.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Bonk.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Loot.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Barrows.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/SecondForm.cs src/Bonkscape.Plugin/Systems/Graveyard.cs -->\n"}, {"section": "wiki", "slug": "monsters-and-bosses", "text": "# Monsters and bosses\n\n## How a monster is made\n\nEach OSRS monster takes over one or more Megabonk enemies: their spawn slots, movement and attacks. Bonkscape draws\nit with its **OSRS NPC model** from your cache (the six Barrows brothers instead as an OSRS body in\ntheir own Barrows armour, since their NPC models are pink in the cache), lit the way the OSRS client lights it, and plays its OSRS\n[animations](#animations). Megabonk's own model is hidden. The OSRS model follows the enemy, feet on the ground, and\nturns to face you.\n\nFor ordinary monsters, Bonkscape also replaces Megabonk's **HP, damage, speed and knockback resistance** with the\nrow's values. **Minibosses and bosses keep Megabonk's own stats**, so the Giant Mole fights with the HP and damage of\nthe Megabonk boss it replaces, not the sheet's 9000 HP (TzTok-Jad, the Kalphite Queen, the King Black Dragon and the God Wars\ngenerals: not theirs). A map's difficulty (`maps.difficulty`) scales HP and damage for all of them; every map's is 1. Each row joins the spawn pool of its own maps (the roster's **Maps** column): Lumbridge's,\nArdougne & Seers' Village's and the God Wars Dungeon's monsters on Megabonk's Forest; Al Kharid's, Morytania's and TzHaar City's on the Desert; the Wilderness's on the Graveyard. The maps of one Megabonk\nmap share its enemy types: when a stage starts, its map's rows take the types over (names, models and stats). The Fight\nCaves' monsters (Maps `tztok_jad_boss`) join no pool; the arena spawns them itself. Megabonk enemies that no row takes\nover are removed from those maps' pools, so only OSRS monsters spawn on Bonkscape's maps. On a later\nstage, Megabonk's own stage scaling multiplies every monster's HP and damage on top, as it does in any run.\n\n## Roster\n\n<!-- gen:enemies -->\n| Monster | OSRS NPC (id) | Stand-in NPC ids | Combat | Style | Megabonk enemies | HP | Damage | Speed | Knockback resist | Size x | Maps | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Chicken** | Chicken (1173) | 1174, 2804, 2805, 1175 | 1 | melee | Slime | 6 | 8 | 6.5 | 0 | 1 | lumbridge | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Goblin** | Goblin (3031) | 3029, 655, 657, 659, 674, 2484 | 2 | melee | Goblin, GoblinStrong | 7 | 15 | 5 | 0 | 1 | lumbridge | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Giant rat** | Giant rat (2510) | 2511, 2512, 2856, 2492, 1020 | 3 | melee | Ghoul | 9 | 12 | 9 | 0 | 1 | lumbridge | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Guard** | Guard (3010) | 3011, 1546, 1547, 1548, 995, 397 | 21 | ranged | Skeleton | 20 | 20 | 5.5 | 0.1 | 1 | lumbridge | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Cow** | Cow (2790) | 2791, 2793, 2795 | 2 | melee | ArmoredSkeleton, GoblinTank, MinibossPig | 30 | 22 | 4 | 0.3 | 1.1 | lumbridge | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Giant frog** | Giant frog (477) | 478 | 99 | magic | Golem, MinibossGolem | 20 | 20 | 5 | 0.2 | 1.6 | lumbridge | <span class=\"st st-verified\">verified</span> |\n| **Giant Mole** | Giant Mole (5779) | 6499 | 230 | melee | Ent1, Ent2, Ent3 | 9000 | 40 | 7 | 1 | 2.2 | lumbridge | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Myre blamish snail** | Myre Blamish Snail (2634) | 2649 | 9 | melee | Mummy | 8 | 13 | 5.5 | 0 | 1 | morytania | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Bat** | Bat (2827) | 10888, 14042 | 6 | melee | Tumblebone | 7 | 9 | 11 | 0 | 1 | morytania | <span class=\"st st-built\">built</span> |\n| **Crawling Hand** | Crawling Hand (448) | 